n\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has dimensions \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct rows.\n\nSimilarly, for each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i=\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{i\\},\n\\]\n\nwith dimensions \\((n-i)\\times 1\\).  \nThese rectangles are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct columns.\n\nObserve that:\n\n* Every square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) lies in exactly one of the rectangles \\(R_i\\) (its row is \\(i\\) and its column belongs to \\(\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)).  \n* Every square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\) lies in exactly one of the rectangles \\(S_j\\) (its column is \\(j\\) and its row belongs to \\(\\{j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)).  \n\nThus the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}=\\{R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1},\\,S_1,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers **all** unit squares of the board except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  The rectangles are axis‑parallel, their interiors are disjoint, and each rectangle’s sides lie on grid lines.  Consequently \\(\\mathcal{T}\\) is a valid tiling consisting of  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{T}|=2(n-1)\n\\]\n\ntiles.  For \\(n=2025\\) this yields \\(2\\cdot2024=4048\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound: at least \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nConsider the set of squares immediately **above** the diagonal:\n\n\\[\nD^{+}=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* No rectangle of the allowed type can contain two distinct squares of \\(D^{+}\\).\n\n*Proof of claim.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) (with \\(I,J\\) intervals of rows and columns, respectively) contains \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThen \\(i,j\\in I\\) and \\(i+1,j+1\\in J\\).  \nSince \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals, we have \\(\\max I\\ge j\\) and \\(\\min J\\le i+1\\).  \nBecause the rectangle must avoid all diagonal squares, we require \\(\\max I<\\