le n\\},\n     \\]  \n\n     i.e. rows \\(1\\) through \\(k\\) and columns \\(k+1\\) through \\(n\\).  \n     These \\(n-1\\) tiles fill the region right of the diagonal.  \n\n   \u2013 No tile contains a diagonal square, and every non\u2011diagonal unit square belongs to exactly one of the constructed rectangles.  \n   \u2013 The total number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   \u2013 The lower\u2011bound argument shows that no arrangement can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n   \u2013 The explicit construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n   \u2013 Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   \u2013 For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  A placement of tiles satisfying the requirement leaves exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  Denote the uncovered squares by  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Every unit square not belonging to \\(U\\) must be covered by exactly one rectangular tile whose sides lie on grid lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nSplit the board into the two disjoint regions  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\]\n\nthe squares lying respectively **left** of and **right** of the uncovered square in their row.  A tile cannot intersect a square of \\(U\\); consequently each tile is wholly contained in either \\(L\\) or \\(R\\).  Hence the total number of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}= \\#\\text{tiles covering }L+\\#\\text{tiles covering }R .\n\\]\n\nWe treat \\(L\\); the same argument applies to \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.1  Geometry of \\(L\\)\n\nFor a fixed column \\(j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n\\)) a square \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) iff \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\).  Since the values \\(\\sigma(i)\\) run through all numbers \\(1,\\dots ,n\\), exactly the \\(n-j\\) rows whose \\(\\sigma(i)\\) are larger than \\(j\\) contain a square in column \\(j\\).  Thus\n\n\\[\n\\text{column }j\\text{ of }L\\text{ has height }n-j .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the non\u2011empty columns of \\(L\\) are \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) and their heights form the strictly decreasing sequence  \n\n\\[\nn-1,\\;n-2,\\;\\dots ,\\;1 .\n\\]\n\nThe rows of \\(L\\) have lengths \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\); these lengths are a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.2  Outer\u2011corner cells\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consider the cell  \n\n\\[\nc_k=(k,\\;n-k).\n\\]\n\nBecause column \\(n-k\\) has height \\(k\\) by (1), the cell \\(c_k\\) is the **lowest** cell of its column.  Moreover row \\(k\\) has length at least \\(n-k\\) (since \\(\\sigma(k)\\ge n-k+1\\)), so \\(c_k\\) is also the **right\u2011most** cell of its row.  Hence each \\(c_k\\) is an *outer corner* of the region \\(L\\).\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such corners, namely \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_{n-1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.3  One corner per rectangle\n\nLet \\(R\\) be any axis\u2011parallel rectangle contained in \\(L\\).  Suppose \\(R\\) contained two distinct outer corners, say \\(c_{k}\\) and \\(c_{\\ell}\\) with \\(k<\\ell\\).  Then \\(R\\) would contain the cell \\((\\ell,\\,n-k)\\), which lies **to the right** of column \\(n-\\ell\\) and **below** row \\(k\\).  By construction this cell is **not** in \\(L\\) (it lies beyond the right end of row \\(\\ell\\) or below the bottom of column \\(n-k\\)).  Hence a rectangle in \\(L\\) cannot contain two distinct outer corners.\n\nTherefore each rectangle covering part of \\(L\\) can contain **at most one** of the \\(n-1\\) outer\u2011corner cells.  Since all squares of \\(L\\) must be covered, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary for \\(L\\).\n\nThe same reasoning applied to the right\u2011hand region  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}\n\\]\n\n(which is the mirror image of \\(L\\)) shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(R\\).\n\nConsequently any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction using exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); i.e. leave the squares on the main diagonal uncovered:\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe region left of the diagonal is  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\n\\]\n\nand the region right of the diagonal is  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth regions are the familiar lower\u2011 and upper\u2011triangular halves of the board.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Tiling the lower\u2011triangular region\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) place a tile covering the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k^{\\text{L}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe rectangle \\(T_k^{\\text{L}}\\) has height \\(n-k\\) and width \\(k\\); it lies completely inside \\(L\\) because every row \\(i\\ge k+1\\) satisfies \\(i>k\\ge j\\).  The family \\(\\{T_k^{\\text{L}}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) is pairwise disjoint and their union equals \\(L\\).  Thus \\(L\\) is tiled by exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  Tiling the upper\u2011triangular region\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) place a tile covering  \n\n\\[\nT_k^{\\text{R}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHere the rectangle has height \\(k\\) and width \\(n-k\\); it is wholly contained in \\(R\\)