false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nConsider a $2025\\times2025$ grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile.\n\nDetermine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of rectangular tiles satisfying the condition leaves exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the diagonal case  \n\nApply the row permutation \\(\\pi\\) defined by \\(\\pi(i)=\\sigma(i)\\) (i.e. move row \\(i\\) to position \\(\\sigma(i)\\)).  \nColumns are left unchanged.  \nUnder this transformation the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) moves to  \n\n\\[\n(\\pi(i),\\sigma(i))=(\\sigma(i),\\sigma(i)),\n\\]\n\nwhich lies on the main diagonal.  \nEvery rectangle is mapped to a rectangle of the same size, and the number of rectangles is unchanged.  \nTherefore the minimum number of tiles for an arbitrary permutation \\(\\sigma\\) equals the minimum number for the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\).  \nHenceforth we assume  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nWith (2) the set of covered squares splits into  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\},\n\\]\n\nthe lower‑ and upper‑triangular halves of the board.\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the two *outer‑corner* cells  \n\n\\[\nc_k^{L}=(k+1,k)\\in L,\\qquad \nc_k^{R}=(k,k+1)\\in R .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\{c_k^{L},c_k^{R}\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consists of exactly \\(2n-2\\) distinct covered squares.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet \\(Q\\) be an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior contains no diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\).  \nThen \\(Q\\) contains at most one of the cells listed in (3).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(Q=I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\) is a non‑empty interval of rows and \\(J\\) a non‑empty interval of columns.\n\n*Case 1 – two corners of \\(L\\).*  \nSuppose \\(Q\\) contains \\(c_i^{L}=(i+1,i)\\) and \\(c_j^{L}=(j+1,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThen \\(I\\) contains the rows \\(i+1,\\dots ,j+1\\) and \\(J\\) contains the columns \\(i,\\dots ,j\\).  \nConsequently the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) (row \\(i+1\\), column \\(i+1\\)) lies in \\(I\\times J\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \n\n*Case 2 – two corners of \\(R\\).*  \nIf \\(Q\\) contains \\(c_i^{R}=(i,i+1)\\) and \\(c_j^{R}=(j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\), then \\(I\\) contains rows \\(i,\\dots ,j\\) and \\(J\\) contains columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,j+1\\).  \nHence the diagonal cell \\((j,j)\\) belongs to \\(Q\\), again a contradiction.  \n\n*Case 3 – one corner of \\(L\\) and one of \\(R\\).*  \nAssume \\(Q\\) contains \\(c_i^{L}=(i+1,i)\\) and \\(c_j^{R}=(j,j+1)\\).  \nIf \\(i\\le j\\), then \\(I\\) contains rows \\(i+1,\\dots ,j\\) and \\(J\\) contains columns \\(i,\\dots ,j+1\\); the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) lies in \\(Q\\).  \nIf \\(i>j\\), then \\(I\\) contains rows \\(j,\\dots ,i+1\\) and \\(J\\) contains columns \\(j,\\dots ,i\\); the diagonal cell \\((j+1,j+1)\\) lies in \\(Q\\).  \nIn both sub‑cases a diagonal cell is present, contradicting the hypothesis. ∎\n\n---\n\nBecause each rectangle can contain at most one of the \\(2n-2\\) outer‑corner cells, any tiling of the board that avoids the diagonal must use at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge 2n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nKeep the uncovered squares on the diagonal as in (2).\n\n*Tiles covering the lower‑triangular region.*  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k^{L}= \\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n\\(T_k^{L}\\) has height \\(n-k\\) and width \\(k\\); it lies entirely inside \\(L\\) because every row \\(i\\ge k+1\\) satisfies \\(j\\le k< i\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T_k^{L}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) is pairwise disjoint and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{k=1}^{n-1} T_k^{L}=L .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Tiles covering the upper‑triangular region.*  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k^{R}= \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\\(T_k^{R}\\) has height \\(k\\) and width \\(n-k\\); it lies inside \\(R\\) because for every row \\(i\\le k\\) we have \\(j\\ge k+1>i\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T_k^{R}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) is pairwise disjoint and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{k=1}^{n-1} T_k^{R}=R .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNo tile contains a diagonal cell, and every non‑diagonal unit square belongs to exactly one tile.  \nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Minimality\n\nInequality (4) shows that no admissible placement can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nConstruction (5)–(9) achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** The minimum number of tiles Matilda must place is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"