nnot be both a prefix and a suffix (its row interval would then be the whole set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and would contain a hole), the left region requires **at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles**.\n\n   *For the right region* the situation is symmetric, giving the same bound.  \n   Consequently any tiling of the whole board needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   - For each column \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-1)\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((i,c)\\) with \\(i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   - For each row \\(r\\;(1\\le r\\le n-1)\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells \\((r,j)\\) with \\(j=r+1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n\n   These are \\(n-1\\) vertical and \\(n-1\\) horizontal rectangles, all disjoint, none containing a hole, and together they cover every square except the \\(n\\) diagonal holes. Hence a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote by \\(\\sigma\\in S_n\\) the permutation defined by the uncovered squares:\n\\[\n\\text{the uncovered square in row }i\\text{ is }(i,\\sigma(i)).\n\\]\nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection.\n\nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; it may not contain any uncovered square.  \nConsequently a tile can intersect only cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq\\sigma(i)\\).  \nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) the admissible columns split into two intervals:\n\\[\nL_i=\\{j:1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{j:\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nThus every tile is either a **left‑tile** (its column interval is contained in \\(\\bigcap_{i\\in I}L_i\\) for the rows \\(I\\) it covers) o