ing this part must have bottom row \\(\\le a\\); if it were \\(<a\\) some row of the prefix would be missed, while a bottom row \\(>a\\) would contain the forbidden hole \\((a+1,c)\\).  Hence a prefix‑tile with bottom row exactly \\(a\\) is required. ∎\n\nBy Lemma 1 we need a distinct prefix‑tile for each bottom row \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\); therefore at least \\(n-1\\) prefix‑tiles are necessary.\n\nAn entirely symmetric argument applied to the suffix parts of the sets \\(S_c\\) yields:\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nAt least \\(n-1\\) suffix‑tiles are necessary to cover the suffix parts of the columns.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the rows from the bottom.  For each \\(b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is a column whose hole lies in row \\(b-1\\); its suffix part is \\(\\{b,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and must be covered by a suffix‑tile whose top row is exactly \\(b\\).  Hence we need a distinct suffix‑tile for each \\(b=2,\\dots ,n\\), i.e. \\(n-1\\) of them. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nNo tile can be simultaneously a prefix‑tile and a suffix‑tile.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA prefix‑tile has its rows of the form \\(\\{1,\\dots ,a\\}\\) for some \\(a<n\\); a suffix‑tile has rows \\(\\{b,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(b>1\\).  \nIf a rectangle were both, its row set would have to be both a prefix and a suffix, which forces \\(\\{1,\\dots ,a\\}=\\{b,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  This is impossible because the two sets are disjoint unless \\(a=n\\) and \\(b=1\\), i.e. the whole set of rows.  The whole set would contain every hole, contradicting the definition of a tile. ∎\n\nFrom Lemmas 1–3 we conclude that the left region \\(L\\) requires at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\ntiles.  The same reasoning applied to the right region \\(R\\) gives the identical bound, but the tiles of \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint, so (2) already counts all tiles needed for the whole board.  \nThus **an