ectangle, and the row intervals of those two rectangles are disjoint.  \n- The union of all \\(T_j^{\\text{low}}\\) and \\(T_j^{\\text{up}}\\) is precisely the set of all unit squares except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  \n\nThus we have constructed a tiling with  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{T_j^{\\text{low}}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{T_j^{\\text{up}}\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Lower bound  \n\nFix any permutation \\(\\sigma\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) define its **left interval**\n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n(If \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) the interval is empty.)  \nAll squares left of the holes are exactly \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\).\n\nA tile that contains any square of a left interval cannot contain a square to the right of the hole in the same row; consequently every tile covering left squares is a *left tile*: its column interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) satisfies \\(c_2\\le \\sigma(i)-1\\) for every row \\(i\\) belonging to the tile.  \n\nLet \\(a_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\) (so \\(a_i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and the multiset \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)).  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is a unique row  \n\n\\[\nr_k\\quad\\text{with}\\quad a_{r_k}=k\\qquad(\\text{equivalently }\\sigma(r_k)=k+1).\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((r_k,k)\\) belongs to the left region, and it is the **highest** cell of column \\(k\\) in that region (all rows with a left interval containing column \\(k\\) are precisely those rows \\(i\\) with \\(a_i\\ge k\\); among them \\(r_k\\) has the smallest \\(a_i\\)).  \n\nConsider any left tile \\(R\\) covering the cell \\((r_k,k)\\).  \nBecause \\(R\\) is a left tile, its column interval must be of the form \\([c_1,c_2]\\) with \\(c_2\\le a_{r_k}=k\\).  \nSince \\((r_k,k)\\) lies in \\(R\\), we have \\(c_2\\ge k\\); therefore \\(c_2=k\\) and the column interval of \\(R\\) is contained in \\([c_1,k]\\).\n\nNow take two distinct indices \\(k<\\ell\\).  \nIf a single left tile were to contain both \\((r_k,k)\\) and \\((r_\\ell,\\ell)\\), its column interval would have to contain both columns \\(k\\) and \\(\\ell\\); consequently its rightmost column would be at least \\(\\ell\\).  \nBut the row \\(r_k\\) has left‑interval length \\(a_{r_k}=k\\), so any left tile containing row \\(r_k\\) cannot extend beyond column \\(k\\).  \nHence no left tile can contain two different cells \\((r_k,k)\\) and \\((r_\\ell,\\ell)\\).  \n\nThus the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\{(r_k,k)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) each require a distinct left tile.  \nConsequently any tiling needs at least \\(n-1\\) left tiles.\n\nBy reflecting the board (interchanging rows and columns) the same argument applies to the **right** region (the squares to the right of each hole).  \nHence at least another \\(n-1\\) right tiles are necessary.\n\nTherefore any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Minimality  \n\nThe construction of §1 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the argument of §2 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
