"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   \u2013 Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is \\(\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (here \\(n=2025\\)).  \n\n2. **Left/right classification of tiles.**  \n   \u2013 A tile that contains a square left of the hole in some row cannot contain a square right of that hole (otherwise the rectangle would also contain the hole).  \n   \u2013 Hence every tile is either a **left tile** (all its squares lie left of the hole in each of its rows) or a **right tile** (all its squares lie right of the hole in each of its rows).  \n\n3. **Lower bound \\(2n-2\\).**  \n   \u2013 For each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is a unique row \\(i_c\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_c)=c+1\\).  \n   \u2013 The cell \\((i_c,c)\\) lies left of its hole and must be covered by a left tile. Because the tile cannot reach column \\(c+1\\), its rightmost column is exactly \\(c\\).  \n   \u2013 Thus every column \\(c\\) ( \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) ) is the rightmost column of at least one left tile. Since a tile has a single rightmost column, at least \\(n-1\\) left tiles are required.  \n   \u2013 By reflecting the board (or the same argument with columns \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and rows where \\(\\sigma(j_c)=c-1\\)) we obtain that at least \\(n-1\\) right tiles are needed.  \n   \u2013 Consequently any admissible tiling uses at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining \\(2n-2\\).**  \n   \u2013 Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\).  \n   \u2013 For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     T_j^{\\text{low}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     which lies entirely below the hole \\((j,j)\\).  \n   \u2013 For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     T_j^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\},\n     \\]\n     which lies entirely above the hole \\((j,j)\\).  \n   \u2013 The two tiles in a given column are disjoint (the hole separates them), and tiles in different columns are disjoint because they occupy different columns.  \n   \u2013 Their union covers every unit square except the diagonal holes \\((i,i)\\).  \n   \u2013 The construction uses \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   \u2013 The lower\u2011bound argument shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n   \u2013 For \\(n=2025\\) the minimum number of tiles is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of rectangular tiles on the \\(n\\times n\\) grid is required to satisfy:\n\n* each tile is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines,\n* no unit square is covered by more than one tile,\n* each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nSince each row contains exactly one uncovered square and each column contains exactly one uncovered square, the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix.  \nThus there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) the squares left of the hole are  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\]\n\nand the squares right of the hole are  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the **left region** \\(L=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\) and the **right region** \\(R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i\\).  \nEvery unit square not in \\(U\\) belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Every tile is wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\)\n\nLet \\(T\\) be a tile covering some square \\((i,j)\\in L\\).  \nIf \\(T\\) also contained a square \\((i,k)\\) with \\(k>\\sigma(i)\\) (i.e. a square in \\(R\\) of the same row), then because \\(T\\) is a rectangle it would contain all columns between \\(j\\) and \\(k\\), in particular column \\(\\sigma(i)\\). Hence \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\in T\\), contradicting the fact that the hole is uncovered.  \nTherefore a tile that contains any square of \\(L\\) cannot contain any square of \\(R\\).  \nConsequently each tile is either a **left tile** (a subset of \\(L\\)) or a **right tile** (a subset of \\(R\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the number of left tiles  \n\nConsider a column index \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, there exists a unique row  \n\n\\[\ni_c\\quad\\text{such that}\\quad\\sigma(i_c)=c+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((i_c,c)\\) lies in \\(L\\) (it is left of the hole \\((i_c,c+1)\\)) and therefore must be covered by some left tile, say \\(T\\).  \nWrite the column interval of \\(T\\) as \\([a,b]\\) with \\(a\\le b\\).  \nSince \\((i_c,c)\\in T\\) we have \\(a\\le c\\le b\\).  \nIf \\(b\\ge c+1\\) then column \\(c+1\\) would also belong to \\(T\\), and because \\(\\sigma(i_c)=c+1\\) the hole \\((i_c,c+1)\\) would be covered\u2014impossible.  \nHence \\(b\\le c\\).  \nCombined with \\(c\\le b\\) we obtain \\(b=c\\).  \n\nThus **the rightmost column of the left tile covering \\((i_c,c)\\) is exactly \\(c\\)**.  \nConsequently each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) is the rightmost column of at least one left tile.  \n\nSince a tile has a unique rightmost column, the number of left tiles is at least the number of distinct rightmost columns, i.e. at least \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the number of right tiles  \n\nApply the same reasoning to the right region.  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) there is a unique row  \n\n\\[\nj_c\\quad\\text{such that}\\quad\\sigma(j_c)=c-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((j_c,c)\\) belongs to \\(R\\) and must be covered by a right tile.  \nAn analogous argument shows that the **leftmost column of that right tile is exactly \\(c\\)**.  \nHence each column \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the leftmost column of at least one right tile, and therefore at least \\(n-1\\) right tiles are required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Total lower bound  \n\nCombining the two parts,\n\n\\[\n\\text{(number of tiles)}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\)).  \n\n*For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\)* define the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{low}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt occupies column \\(j\\) and all rows strictly below the hole \\((j,j)\\); therefore it lies entirely in the left region.\n\n*For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\)* define the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt occupies column \\(j\\) and all rows strictly above the hole \\((j,j)\\); therefore it lies entirely in the right region.\n\n**Verification.**  \n\n- In a fixed column \\(j\\) the two tiles \\(T_j^{\\text{up}}\\) and \\(T_j^{\\text{low}}\\) are disjoint because the former uses rows \\(1,\\dots ,j-1\\) while the latter uses rows \\(j+1,\\dots ,n\\); the hole \\((j,j)\\) separates them.  \n- Tiles belonging to different columns use different columns, so they are disjoint as well.  \n- Every unit square not on the diagonal belongs either to some \\(T_j^{\\text{up}}\\) (if it lies above the diagonal) or to some \\(T_j^{\\text{low}}\\) (if it lies below the diagonal). Hence all non\u2011diagonal squares are covered exactly once.  \n\nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{T_j^{\\text{low}}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{T_j^{\\text{up}}\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\,|\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while construction (2) exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles. Hence the bound is tight.\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
