ntain two left‑neighbour squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 3.** \\(\\varepsilon_i=-1,\\;\\varepsilon_j=+1\\) (one left, one right neighbour).  \n\nHere \\(c_1=\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(c_2=\\sigma(j)+1\\).  \nFrom (2) we have \\(c_{\\min}=c_1\\le c_2=c_{\\max}\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=c_1+1\\in[c_1,c_2],\n\\]\n\nand because \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) belongs to \\(R\\), again a contradiction.  \nThe symmetric sub‑case \\(\\varepsilon_i=+1,\\;\\varepsilon_j=-1\\) is analogous.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible pair of distinct squares of \\(S\\) leads to a contradiction, **any rectangle avoiding holes can contain at most one element of \\(S\\).**  \n\nTogether with (1) this yields the lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{At least }2n-2\\text{ tiles are necessary}.} \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThus the holes are the main diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*For the squares **above** the diagonal* (\\(j>i\\)):  \nfor each column \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nC_k=\\{(r,k)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(C_k\\) has size \\((k-1)\\times1\\) and covers precisely the squares of column \\(k\\) that lie above the diagonal.\n\n*For the squares **below** the diagonal* (\\(j<i\\)):  \nfor each row \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(k,c)\\mid 1\\le c\\le k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R_k\\) has size \\(1\\times(k-1)\\) and covers precisely the squares of row \\(k\\) that lie below the diagonal.\n\nThe families \\(\\{C_k\\}_{k=2}^{n}\\) and \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, they avoid all diagonal holes, and together they cover every non‑diagonal square.  \nThe total number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, while construction (4) shows that \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus Matilda must place at least, and can achieve, exactly 4048 rectangular tiles.**"
