 and column \\(n\\) leaves a board of size \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\) whose uncovered squares are given by the restriction of \\(f\\) to the remaining rows; its left region is exactly the set \\(L\\) for the smaller permutation. By the induction hypothesis this smaller left region needs at least \\((n-1)-1=n-2\\) rectangles. Adding the rectangle that covered row \\(i^{*}\\) yields at least \\((n-2)+1=n-1\\) rectangles for the original \\(L\\).\n\n4. **Symmetry for the right region.**  \n   Interchanging rows and columns transforms \\(R\\) into a left region of the transposed permutation, so the same argument shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for \\(R\\).\n\n5. **Upper bound (construction).**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(f(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     V_k=\\{(i,k):i=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     a \\((n-k)\\times1\\) rectangle covering all cells below the diagonal in column \\(k\\).  \n   - For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a horizontal tile  \n     \\[\n     H_k=\\{(k,j):j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     a \\(1\\times (n-k)\\) rectangle covering all cells above the diagonal in row \\(k\\).\n\n   The sets \\(\\{V_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint (different columns), the sets \\(\\{H_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint (different rows), and no vertical tile meets a horizontal tile because a vertical tile lies strictly left of the diagonal while a horizontal tile lies strictly right of it. Hence the \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles cover every unit square except the diagonal squares, and each row and column has exactly one uncovered square.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2(n-1)\\) and the explicit construction achieving it coincide; therefore the minimal possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe board consists of the unit squares \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\}\\) where the first coordinate denotes the row and the second the column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain **exactly one** uncovered square, there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\nf:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,f(i))\\).  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,f(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na permutation matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<f(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>f(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus every unit square not in \\(U\\) belongs either to \\(L\\) (to the left of its row‑hole) or to \\(R\\) (to the right of its row‑hole).\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nNo axis‑parallel rectangle can contain a cell of \\(L\\) and a cell of \\(R\\) simultaneously.  \n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(T\\) contains a cell \\((i,j_{L})\\in L\\) and a cell \\((i,j_{R})\\in R\\) from the same row \\(i\\).  \nThen \\(j_{L}<f(i)<j_{R}\\); the interval of columns of \\(T\\) therefore contains the column \\(f(i)\\), i.e. the hole \\((i,f(i))\\).  \nSince tiles are not allowed to cover a hole, such a rectangle cannot exist. ∎\n\nHence every tile lies completely inside \\(L\\) or completely inside \\(R\\).  \nLet \\(\\tau(L)\\) (resp. \\(\\tau(R)\\)) denote the minimum number of rectangles needed to partition \\(L\\) (resp. \\(R\\)).  \nThe total number of tiles required is \\(\\tau(L)+\\tau(R)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for \\(\\tau(L)\\)\n\nWe prove the following.\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nFor any permutation \\(f\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) one has \\(\\tau(L)\\ge n-1\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*  \n\n**Base \\(n=1\\).**  \n\\(L\\) is empty, so \\(\\tau(L)=0=n-1\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement holds for size \\(n-1\\) and consider size \\(n\\).  \nLet \\(i^{*}\\) be the unique index with \\(f(i^{*})=n\\); the hole in row \\(i^{*}\\) lies in the rightmost column.  \nConsequently the whole set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i^{*},j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\subseteq L\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nbelongs to the left region.\n\nConsider any partition of \\(L\\) into rectangles.  \nThe cell \\((i^{*},n-1)\\) cannot belong to a rectangle that also contains another row:\nif a rectangle \\(R\\) contained a second row \\(i\\neq i^{*}\\), then its column interval would contain \\(n-1\\); but for such a row we have \\(f(i)\\le n-1\\), so column \\(n-1\\) is **not** left of its hole, contradicting Lemma 1.  \nThus \\((i^{*},n-1)\\) lies in a rectangle whose row set is exactly \\(\\{i^{*}\\}\\).  \n\nBecause the cells in (1) are contiguous in row \\(i^{*}\\) and no other rectangle uses any of them, we may enlarge that rectangle leftwards until it covers **all** cells of (1).  \nHence we may assume that there is a rectangle \\(R^{*}\\) covering precisely the set (1).\n\nDelete row \\(i^{*}\\) and column \\(n\\) from the board.  \nThe remaining uncovered squares are \\(\\{(i,f(i))\\mid i\\neq i^{*}\\}\\); they form a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nThe left region of the reduced board is exactly  \n\n\\[\nL'=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\neq i^{*},\\;j<f(i)\\}\\subseteq L\\setminus R^{*}.\n\\]\n\nThus the rectangles covering \\(L\\setminus R^{*}\\) constitute a partition of \\(L'\\).  \nBy the induction hypothesis, \\(\\tau(L')\\ge (n-1)-1=n-2\\).  \n\nAdding the rectangle \\(R^{*}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\tau(L)\\ge \\tau(L')+1\\ge (n-2)+1=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for \\(\\tau(R)\\)\n\nThe board is symmetric with respect to transposition (exchange rows and columns).  \nTransposing interchanges \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) while preserving the property “each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square”.  \nTherefore the same argument applied to the transposed board yields  \n\n\\[\n\\tau(R)\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Consequence for the whole board  \n\nEvery admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n\\tau(L)+\\tau(R)\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A tiling attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(f(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\)).  \nDefine the following rectangles.\n\n*Vertical tiles (covering the left side of the diagonal):* for each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\),\n\n\\[\nV_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_k\\) is a \\((n-k)\\times1\\) rectangle; all its cells satisfy \\(k< i\\), i.e. they lie left of the diagonal and are not holes.\n\n*Horizontal tiles (covering the right side of the diagonal):* for each \\(k=2,3,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\nH_k=\\{(k,j)\\mid j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_k\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-k)\\) rectangle; all its cells satisfy \\(j>k\\), i.e. they lie right of the diagonal and are not holes.\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n- **Axis‑parallel and on grid lines:** each \\(V_k\\) and \\(H_k\\) is a product of integer intervals, hence a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.  \n- **Disjointness:** the vertical tiles occupy distinct columns, the horizontal tiles occupy distinct rows, and a vertical tile lies entirely in columns \\(\\le k\\) while a horizontal tile lies in rows \\(\\ge k\\); therefore no vertical tile meets any horizontal tile.  \n- **Coverage:** Every cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) satisfies either \\(j<i\\) (belongs to \\(V_j\\)) or \\(j>i\\) (belongs to \\(H_i\\)). Hence all non‑diagonal squares are covered exactly once.  \n- **Uncovered squares:** The only uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\); each row and each column contains precisely one of them.\n\nThe number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{V_k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{H_k\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}\\,|\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (2) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while construction (3) shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimal possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
