rizontal tile covering squares to the right of the diagonal; for each column j, a vertical tile covering squares below the diagonal. That yields 2n-2 tiles, covering all squares except diagonal. This seems plausible. Is it minimal? The lower bound argument seems correct. So answer 4048 is correct.\n\nThus Level 1: answer is correct.\n\nNow Level 2: Check reasoning validity. The solution uses a combinatorial argument with monotone lattice path, defines set C, shows each tile can contain at most one element of C, thus lower bound. Then constructs a covering. The methods used: combinatorial counting, construction, case analysis (implicitly). These are valid mathematical methods. However, we need to check the \"critical level 2 enhancement\" about construction claims: The solution includes a construction. Does it provide concrete details? Let's see.\n\nIn Section 4, \"Construction attaining the bound\", they define identity permutation p(i)=i. Then define horizontal tiles H_i = { (i,j) | i < j ≤ n }, a 1×(n-i) rectangle. That's an explicit specification: a rectangle covering row i from column i+1 to n. Similarly vertical tiles V_j = { (i,j) | j < i ≤ n }, an (n-j)×1 rectangle covering column j from row j+1 to n. These are explicit definitions of rectangles. So they provide concrete details (explicit enumeration of squares). This qualifies as Category C (explicit specification). So Level 2 passes.\n\nThus Level 2: reasoning uses valid methods, and construction provides concrete details, so Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. We need to check for any justification gaps or critical errors. The solution is fairly thorough. However, we need to examine if there are any missing details or imprecise statements that could be considered justification gaps. The solution defines set C and claims that each tile can contain at most one element of C, and provides a proof by case analysis. The proof uses notation (L) and (R) but there is a small mismatch: earlier they