ans all columns < all rows (c2 < r1). That is correct.\n\nThus any rectangle above diagonal cannot contain two cells (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i<j. The proof seems valid.\n\nThus lower bound 2n-2 stands.\n\nNow construction: They define for each i from 1 to n-1, a horizontal rectangle H_i covering row i, columns i+1..n. That's a 1 x (n-i) rectangle. For each j from 1 to n-1, a vertical rectangle V_j covering column j, rows j+1..n. That's (n-j) x 1 rectangle.\n\nThese rectangles are disjoint: each H_i occupies row i only, each V_j occupies column j only. Intersection would be at cell (i,j) where i = row of H_i, j = column of V_j. For that cell to be in both, we need i = row index of V_j (i.e., i > j) and j = column index of H_i (j > i). Contradiction. So they are disjoint.\n\nNow coverage: For any off-diagonal cell (i,j) with i<j, it's in H_i because H_i covers row i, columns i+1..n, which includes j. For i>j, it's in V_j because V_j covers column j, rows j+1..n, which includes i. So all off-diagonal cells are covered exactly once (since each cell belongs either to a unique H_i or V_j, not both). Diagonal cells (i,i) are not covered because H_i covers columns > i only, V_i covers rows > i only, so diagonal cells are uncovered. So we have exactly n uncovered squares, one per row and column.\n\nThus the construction uses (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2 rectangles. So the lower bound matches the construction, establishing minimal number = 2n-2 = 4048.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow we need to check the reasoning validity: The solution uses valid methods: case analysis, combinatorial reasoning, proof by contradiction (in Lemma 2), explicit construction. All methods are recognized valid.\n\nNow we need to check if any construction claims lack concrete details. The solution provides explicit definitions of H_i and V_j. That's concrete: they specify sets of cells. So it's Category C (valid method). So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: presentation issues. Are there any justification gap