ed square of each row it occupies; analogously a rectangle placed\ninside \\(R\\) must be completely to the right of the uncovered square of each\nrow it occupies.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the number of tiles in \\(L\\)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nc_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\n\\]\n\nis the **rightmost** covered cell of that row (it lies in \\(L\\)).  \nSuppose two distinct rows \\(i\\neq i'\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(i')\\) are covered\nby the same rectangle \\(T\\subseteq L\\).  \nBecause \\(T\\) contains \\(c_{i'}\\), its rightmost column is at most\n\\(\\sigma(i')-1\\).  But \\(c_i\\) lies in column \\(\\sigma(i)-1>\\sigma(i')-1\\), so\n\\(c_i\\notin T\\) – a contradiction.  Hence **no rectangle can contain two\nrightmost cells belonging to different rows**.\n\nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\); all other \\(n-1\\) rows have a rightmost\ncell in \\(L\\).  Consequently each of these \\(n-1\\) cells must be covered by a\ndifferent rectangle, and therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,|{\\cal T}_L|\\ge n-1\\,},\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\({\\cal T}_L\\) denotes the set of tiles placed inside \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the number of tiles in \\(R\\)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nd_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\n\\]\n\nis the **leftmost** covered cell of that row (it lies in \\(R\\)).  \nIf a rectangle \\(T\\subseteq R\\) contained two such cells belonging to rows\n\\(i\\neq i'\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(i')\\), then \\(T\\) would have to start\nto the right of column \\(\\sigma(i')\\) (to avoid the uncovered square in row\n\\(i'\\)), while also containing column \\(\\sigma(i)+1<\\sigma(i')\\); this is\nimpossible.  Hence each leftmost cell must lie in a distinct rectangle.\n\nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); all other \\(n-1\\) rows have a leftmost 