red squares are diagonal. Then left region: for each column j from 1 to n-1, they take L_j = { (i,j) : i=j+1,...,n }. That's a vertical strip (a column segment from row j+1 down to n). That's a rectangle: columns j to j, rows j+1 to n. That's a rectangle of size (n - j) x 1. Similarly right region: for each column j from 2 to n, R_j = { (i,j) : i=1,...,j-1 } which is a vertical strip from row 1 to j-1 in column j. That's also a rectangle. So they have n-1 tiles in left region and n-1 tiles in right region, total 2n-2. This covers all off-diagonal cells exactly once. Indeed each off-diagonal cell (i,j) with i != j belongs either to left region if j < i (i.e., column j left of diagonal) or right region if j > i (i.e., column j right of diagonal). In left region, each column j has rows i > j; they are covered by tile L_j. In right region, each column j has rows i < j; they are covered by tile R_j. So each off-diagonal cell is covered exactly once. So the construction works.\n\nThus answer 4048 is correct.\n\nNow we need to check reasoning validity. The solution uses valid methods: permutation representation, case analysis, counting arguments, contradiction (for Lemma 3.1 and 4.1). They also provide a construction with explicit rectangles. So the reasoning uses valid mathematical principles.\n\nNow we need to check presentation quality: any justification gaps? The solution is fairly rigorous. However, we need to see if any steps are missing details that could be considered gaps. Let's examine each part.\n\n- They define L and R sets and claim each tile lies entirely in L or R. The proof is clear: they consider a tile covering rows i0...i1 and columns a...b. For each row k in that range, uncovered square is (k, sigma(k)). Since tile cannot cover that, either b < sigma(k) for all k, or a > sigma(k) for all k. They argue that because a,b are same for all rows, the inequality must hold for all rows with same orientation. This is correct. However, there is a subtlety: they n