tal number of tiles below 2n-2.\n\nWe need to see if there exists a tiling with fewer than 2n-2 tiles that uses some crossing tiles. The solution's construction uses 2n-2 tiles, all vertical, each tile wholly in L or R. Could we do better? Let's explore.\n\nThe problem: we have n uncovered squares forming a permutation matrix. We need to cover all other squares with rectangles (tiles) without overlapping and without covering uncovered squares. The goal is to minimize number of tiles.\n\nThe solution's lower bound argument might be flawed if crossing tiles can reduce the total number of tiles below 2n-2. Let's examine if crossing tiles can reduce the count.\n\nWe need to find a lower bound on total number of tiles. The solution's lower bound is based on each column j (1..n-1) forcing a distinct tile covering the rightmost covered square in column j on the left side. However, if a tile crosses both sides, it could cover that rightmost covered square (i_j, j) while also covering some squares on the right side. But the tile would then not be a subset of L, so the argument that each tile covering (i_j, j) must be a tile in L fails. However, the argument that each column j forces a distinct tile covering (i_j, j) still holds regardless of whether the tile also covers squares in R. Because any tile covering (i_j, j) must have rightmost column exactly j, as we argued. So each column j forces a distinct tile (with rightmost column j). This tile could also cover squares in R. But then the total number of tiles is at least n-1 (since each column j yields a distinct tile). Similarly, each column j (2..n) forces a distinct tile covering leftmost covered square in column j on the right side, with leftmost column exactly j. So we have at least n-1 tiles for right side. However, could the same tile serve both purposes? Could a tile have rightmost column j and leftmost column j' for some j and j'? Wait, a tile cannot have both rightmost column j and leftmost column j' for j != j'? A