r unit squares are to be covered by tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Two families of critical cells  \n\nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)>1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1) .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) is the right‑most covered cell that lies **left** of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_i\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n-1\\) cells, because precisely one row has \\(\\pi(i)=1\\).\n\nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)<n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1) .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(R_i\\) is the left‑most covered cell that lies **right** of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{R_i\\mid \\pi(i)<n\\}\n\\]\n\nalso contains exactly \\(n-1\\) cells (the unique row with \\(\\pi(i)=n\\) contributes none).\n\nObserve that the columns of the cells in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are the numbers \\(\\pi(i)-1\\;(i\\in\\mathcal L)\\); these are all distinct because the values \\(\\pi(i)\\) are distinct.  Hence the cells of \\(\\mathcal L\\) lie in pairwise different columns.  The same holds for \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma: a rectangle can contain at most one cell of each family  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad \n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{No rectangle can contain two distinct cells of }\\mathcal L,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{No rectangle can contain two distinct cells of }\\mathcal R .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(T\\) contains two distinct cells \\(L_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\(L_j=(j,\\pi(j)-1)\\) of \\(\\mathcal L\\) with \\(\\pi(i)<\\pi(j)\\) (the opposite case is symmetric).  \nLet the column interval of \\(T\\) be \\([c,d]\\).  Since both cells belong to \\(T\\),\n\n\\[\nc\\le \\pi(i)-1\\le d\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad c\\le \\pi(j)-1\\le d .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\pi(i)<\\pi(j)\\) we have \\(\\pi(i)\\le\\pi(j)-1\\le d\\); consequently the