ion).**  \n   For any permutation \\(f\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)  \n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|f(i)-f(i-1)\\bigr|\\ge n-1.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   *Proof.* For each cut between the integers \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\) \\((k=1,\\dots ,n-1)\\) the sequence \\((f(1),\\dots ,f(n))\\) must at some step pass from a value \\(\\le k\\) to a value \\(\\ge k+1\\) (or vice‑versa), because the set contains both a number \\(\\le k\\) (namely \\(1\\)) and a number \\(\\ge k+1\\) (namely \\(n\\)). The absolute difference at that step is at least \\(1\\). Since there are \\(n-1\\) distinct cuts, the sum of absolute differences is at least \\(n-1\\). ∎\n\n   Equality in (2) holds exactly for the monotone permutations \\(f(i)=i\\) (increasing) or \\(f(i)=n+1-i\\) (decreasing).\n\n7. **Consequences.**  \n   Substituting (2) into (1) yields the universal lower bound  \n   \\[\n   T(f)\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Achieving the bound.**  \n   Take the increasing permutation \\(f(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n   - *Left side.* For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) let  \n     \\[\n     L_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n     \\]\n     \\(L_k\\) is a rectangle anchored at the left border; the family \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) is pairwise disjoint and together they cover all cells with \\(j<i\\).  \n   - *Right side.* For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) let  \n     \\[\n     R_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-k,\\;n-k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n     \\]\n     \\(R_k\\) is a rectangle anchored at the right border; the family \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) is pairwise disjoint and together they cover all cells with \\(j>i\\).\n\n   The two families use exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles, they do not overlap, and they leave precisely the diagonal cells uncovered, satisfying the row‑ and column‑condition. Hence the bound (3) is tight.\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe board consists of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered square, there is a function  \n\\[\nf:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,f(i))\\).  \nThe column condition forces \\(f\\) to be injective; hence \\(f\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Decomposition into left and right parts  \n\nDefine\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<f(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>f(i)\\}.\n\\]\nEvery tile is a rectangle whose interior consists solely of cells from \\(L\\) or solely of cells from \\(R\\); a tile cannot contain a hole because the hole lies on a grid line separating the two sets.\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) set\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=f(i)-1\\qquad\\text{(number of cells of \\(L\\) in row \\(i\\))},\\qquad\nr_i:=n-f(i)\\qquad\\text{(number of cells of \\(R\\) in row \\(i\\))}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma 1 – minimal number of left‑anchored rectangles  \n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\ell_0:=0\\). The smallest possible number of axis‑parallel rectangles whose left side coincides with the grid line \\(x=0\\) and which together cover exactly the cells\n\\[\nL'=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le\\ell_i\\}\n\\]\nequals\n\\[\nL_{\\min}= \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\;\\ell_i-\\ell_{i-1}\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the rows in order. When we move from row \\(i-1\\) to row \\(i\\) the available width changes from \\(\\ell_{i-1}\\) to \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n*If \\(\\ell_i>\\ell_{i-1}\\):* the extra \\(\\ell_i-\\ell_{i-1}\\) columns must be covered in row \\(i\\). Since any rectangle is anchored at the left side, each new column must start a new rectangle at row \\(i\\); these rectangles may continue downwards as long as the width of the rows below stays at least that column index. Hence at least \\(\\ell_i-\\ell_{i-1}\\) new rectangles are forced.\n\n*If \\(\\ell_i\\le\\ell_{i-1}\\):* no new rectangle is needed; the rectangles already started in earlier rows can simply stop at row \\(i-1\\).\n\nThus any covering uses at least the number given in (4).  \n\nConversely, a covering with exactly that many rectangles is obtained by the following greedy construction: whenever \\(\\ell_i>\\ell_{i-1}\\) start \\(\\ell_i-\\ell_{i-1}\\) new rectangles at row \\(i\\) (each of width \\(1\\) column, but we may merge columns of equal height later); let each rectangle extend downwards until the first row where the width drops below its column index, at which point the rectangle ends. This construction produces precisely the count (4) and clearly covers all cells of \\(L'\\). ∎\n\nReplacing \\(\\ell_i=f(i)-1\\) and noting \\(\\ell_0=0\\) gives\n\\[\nL_{\\min}= \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\;f(i)-f(i-1)\\},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nwhere we set \\(f(0)=1\\) (so that \\(\\max\\{0,f(1)-f(0)\\}=f(1)-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma 2 – minimal number of right‑anchored rectangles  \n\n*Statement.*  \nDefine \\(r_0:=0\\) (equivalently \\(f(0)=n\\)). The smallest possible number of rectangles whose right side coincides with the grid line \\(x=n\\) and which cover exactly the cells\n\\[\nR'=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;n-r_i+1\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\nequals\n\\[\nR_{\\min}= \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\;r_i-r_{i-1}\\}\n        = \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\;f(i-1)-f(i)\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is symmetric to Lemma 1 after reflecting the board horizontally; the “width’’ of row \\(i\\) on the right side is \\(r_i\\). The same greedy start‑and‑extend procedure yields the formula (6). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound for a fixed permutation  \n\nAdding (5) and (6) and simplifying,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(f)&:=L_{\\min}+R_{\\min}\\\\\n   &=\\bigl(f(1)-1\\bigr)+\\bigl(n-f(1)\\bigr)\n     +\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\Bigl(\\max\\{0,f(i)-f(i-1)\\}\n                         +\\max\\{0,f(i-1)-f(i)\\}\\Bigr)\\\\[2mm]\n   &= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|f(i)-f(i-1)\\bigr|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus any tiling respecting a permutation \\(f\\) uses at least the number of tiles given by (7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Lemma 3 – variation of a permutation  \n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every permutation \\(f\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|f(i)-f(i-1)\\bigr|\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Proof (cut argument).*  \nConsider the \\(n-1\\) cuts between the consecutive integers \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\) \\((k=1,\\dots ,n-1)\\).  \nSince the set \\(\\{f(1),\\dots ,f(n)\\}\\) contains both a number \\(\\le k\\) (namely \\(1\\)) and a number \\(\\ge k+1\\) (namely \\(n\\)), the sequence \\((f(1),\\dots ,f(n))\\) must at some index \\(i\\) pass from a value \\(\\le k\\) to a value \\(\\ge k+1\\) or vice‑versa. The absolute difference \\(|f(i)-f(i-1)|\\) at that step is at least \\(1\\). Because each cut is crossed at least once, the total sum of absolute differences is at least the number of cuts, i.e. \\(n-1\\). ∎\n\nEquality in (8) occurs precisely when the permutation never “backtracks’’ – that is, when the sequence is monotone increasing (\\(f(i)=i\\)) or monotone decreasing (\\(f(i)=n+1-i\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Global lower bound  \n\nInsert (8) into (7):\n\\[\nT(f)\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nHence **any** admissible placement of tiles uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the increasing permutation \\(f(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n\n*Left side.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the rectangle\n\\[\nL_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\\(L_k\\) is anchored at the left border, has height \\(n-k\\) and width \\(k\\).  \nIf a cell \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(j<i\\) then \\(j\\) equals some \\(k\\) with \\(k=j\\) and \\(i\\ge k+1\\); consequently \\((i,j)\\in L_k\\). Hence the family \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) partitions the whole set \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\}\\).\n\n*Right side.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the rectangle\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-k,\\;n-k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(R_k\\) is anchored at the right border, has height \\(n-k\\) and width \\(k\\).  \nIf a cell \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(j>i\\) then set \\(k=n-j\\); we have \\(j=n-k+1\\) and \\(i\\le n-k\\), so \\((i,j)\\in R_k\\). Thus \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) partitions the set \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint, together cover all squares except the diagonal holes, and consist of exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles. Consequently a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) the minimal possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
