ins two distinct points of \\(S\\).  \nIf the two points are \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\), then the row interval of \\(R\\) contains the rows \\(i,i+1,\\dots ,j\\) and the column interval contains the columns \\(i+1,i+2,\\dots ,j+1\\). Consequently the cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) (which lies on the diagonal) belongs to \\(R\\), contradicting the requirement that a tile may not cover a hole.  \n\nIf the two points are \\((i+1,i)\\) and \\((j+1,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\), the same argument shows that \\((i+1,i+1)\\) would be forced into \\(R\\).  \n\nIf one point is of the form \\((i,i+1)\\) and the other \\((j+1,j)\\), then either \\(i\\le j\\) or \\(j\\le i\\); in both cases the rectangle would contain a diagonal cell \\((k,k)\\) for some \\(k\\) between the two rows, again a contradiction.  \n\nThus any admissible rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\). ∎  \n\nBecause the cells of \\(S\\) are pairwise disjoint, covering all of them requires at least \\(|S|=2n-2\\) rectangles. Hence any tiling of the board with uncovered squares on the main diagonal uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\nNow consider an arbitrary permutation \\(f\\).  \nPermuting rows and columns of the board does not change the nature of a rectangle (a rectangle remains a rectangle after any simultaneous permutation of the row indices and of the column indices).  \nApplying the row‑permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) and the column‑permutation \\(\\tau(j)=f(j)\\) sends the holes \\((i,f(i))\\) to the diagonal holes \\((i,i)\\).  \nSince the lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is invariant under such relabellings, it holds for the original permutation as well.\n\nTherefore **any** admissible placement of tiles uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A construction with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nAgain we first treat the identity permutation and then transfer the construction to a general permutation.\n\nAssume the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Left of the diagonal.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_k=\\{(k+1,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times k\\); it occupies precisely the cells of row \\(k+1\\) that lie left of the hole \\((k+1,k+1)\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{H_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles and together they cover all cells with column index smaller than the row index (the whole region below the diagonal).\n\n*Above the diagonal.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_k=\\{(i,k+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\(k\\times1\\); it occupies precisely the cells of column \\(k+1\\) that lie above the hole \\((k+1,k+1)\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{V_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles and together they cover all cells with row index smaller than the column index (the whole region above the diagonal).\n\nThe two families are disjoint (every cell is either strictly below or strictly above the diagonal, never both), and together they cover every unit square except the diagonal holes.  \nThe total number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists for the identity permutation.\n\nFor a general permutation \\(f\\) we apply the inverse row‑ and column‑permutations that sent \\(f\\) to the identity.  \nThese permutations transform the rectangles \\(\\{H_k\\}\\cup\\{V_k\\}\\) into a family of axis‑parallel rectangles that tile the complement of the original set of holes, preserving the number of rectangles.  \nHence a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists for **any** admissible configuration of uncovered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* any admissible tiling needs **at least** \\(2n-2\\) tiles (Section 2);\n* there exists an admissible tiling that uses **exactly** \\(2n-2\\) tiles (Section 3).\n\nConsequently the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\nThus Matilda must place at least \\(4048\\) rectangular tiles, and this number is attainable."
