um possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  Substituting \\(n=2025\\) gives \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  The board consists of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).  \nMatilda chooses a set \\(U\\) of uncovered squares such that each row and each column contains exactly one element of \\(U\\).  \nHence there is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll other squares are to be covered by axis‑aligned rectangular tiles; tiles may not overlap.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A set of “adjacent’’ squares\n\nFor each uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) define  \n\n\\[\nr_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\qquad\\text{if }\\sigma(i)<n,\n\\]\n\\[\nb_i=(i+1,\\sigma(i))\\qquad\\text{if }i<n .\n\\]\n\nThe squares \\(r_i\\) lie immediately to the **right** of the uncovered squares,\nand the squares \\(b_i\\) lie immediately **below** them.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_R=\\{r_i\\mid \\sigma(i)<n\\},\\qquad \nS_B=\\{b_i\\mid i<n\\},\\qquad \nS=S_R\\cup S_B .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause exactly one uncovered square lies in each column, there is precisely one\n\\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); thus \\(|S_R|=n-1\\).  Similarly, exactly one\nuncovered square lies in each row, so \\(|S_B|=n-1\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|S|=|S_R|+|S_B|=2n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lemma: a rectangle contains at most one element of \\(S\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad \n\\text{No axis‑aligned rectangle whose interior contains only covered squares can contain two distinct points of }S .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(R\\) be such a rectangle.  Suppose it contains two distinct points of \\(S\\).\nWe distinguish three possibilities.\n\n1. **Both points belong to \\(S_R\\).**  \n   Let the points be \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) and \\((j,\\sigma(j)+1)\\)