e are n rows and n columns, there are n uncovered squares, one per row and column. So there are exactly n-1 squares to the right of uncovered squares (since exactly one uncovered square is in last column, no right neighbor) and exactly n-1 squares below uncovered squares (since exactly one uncovered square is in last row). So |S| = 2n-2. Good.\n\nNow the construction: identity permutation sigma(i)=i, uncovered squares are diagonal. For each column j (2 ≤ j ≤ n) place vertical tile covering rows 1..j-1 in column j (above diagonal). For each column j (1 ≤ j ≤ n-1) place vertical tile covering rows j+1..n in column j (below diagonal). These are vertical tiles covering all squares not on diagonal. Are they disjoint? Yes, each column j has at most two tiles: one above diagonal, one below diagonal. They don't overlap because they occupy disjoint rows. Tiles are axis-aligned rectangles (vertical strips). Each tile's interior consists only of covered squares (since uncovered squares are only diagonal). So each tile contains at most one element of S: Let's check. For column j, the tile above includes squares (i,j) for i<j. The right neighbor of uncovered square (j,j) is (j,j+1) which is not in column j; the below neighbor is (j+1,j). So S_R and S_B squares are in column j+1 and row j+1 respectively. So each tile above column j contains the squares (i,j) for i<j. Does it contain any element of S? The S_B squares are (i+1, sigma(i)) = (i+1,i) for i < n. So S_B squares are (i+1,i). For column j, the S_B squares in column j are those where sigma(i) = j, i.e., i = sigma^{-1}(j) = j. Actually sigma(i)=i, so sigma(i)=i. So S_B squares are (i+1,i). For column j, we need squares of form (i+1,i) with i=j => (j+1,j). That's in column j, row j+1. That belongs to the tile below diagonal for column j (since tile below covers rows j+1..n). So each tile below contains exactly one S_B square (the one directly below the diagonal). Similarly, each tile above contains exactly one S_R square? S_