.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe vector \\(\\mathbf 1=(1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\) is an eigenvector of \\(J\\) with eigenvalue \\(n\\); any vector orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf 1\\) is an eigenvector with eigenvalue \\(0\\). Hence the eigenvalues of \\(J-I\\) are\n\\[\n\\lambda_1=n-1\\quad(\\text{eigenvector }\\mathbf 1),\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=-1\\quad(\\text{multiplicity }n-1).\n\\]\nAll eigenvalues are non‑zero, so \\(J-I\\) is invertible and therefore has full rank \\(n\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.2 (Boolean rank ≥ real rank).**  \nLet \\(B\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times n}\\). Denote by \\(\\operatorname{br}(B)\\) the smallest integer \\(r\\) for which there exist binary matrices \\(U\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times r}\\) and \\(V\\in\\{0,1\\}^{r\\times n}\\) such that\n\\[\nB=U\\circ V,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\circ\\) denotes Boolean matrix multiplication (entrywise OR of the ordinary products). Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{br}(B)\\ge\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(B).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(B=U\\circ V\\) with the above dimensions, then for each entry\n\\[\nb_{ij}= \\bigvee_{k=1}^{r} (u_{ik}\\wedge v_{kj})\\le \\sum_{k=1}^{r} u_{ik}v_{kj},\n\\]\nso entrywise \\(B\\le UV\\) over the reals. Consequently\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(B)\\le \\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(UV).\n\\]\nThe matrix \\(UV\\) is a sum of \\(r\\) rank‑1 matrices \\(u_k v_k^{\\mathsf T}\\) (where \\(u_k\\) and \\(v_k\\) are the \\(k\\)-th columns of \\(U\\) and rows of \\(V\\)), hence \\(\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(UV)\\le r\\). Combining the inequalities yields\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(B)\\le r.\n\\]\nSince \\(r\\) can be any Boolean decomposition size, the minimal such \\(r\\) satisfies \\(\\operatorname{br}(B)\\ge\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(B)\\). ∎\n\n---\n\nEach rectangular tile used in a tiling is determined by a non‑empty set of rows \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a non‑empty set of columns \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the tile consists of all cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in I\\) and \\(j\\in J\\). Its indicator matrix is precisely the outer product\n\\[\nM_{I,J}= \\mathbf 1_I\\mathbf 1_J^{\\mathsf T},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathbf 1_I\\) (resp. \\(\\mathbf 1_J\\)) is the binary column (resp. row) vector indicating the rows (resp. columns) in the set. Hence each tile contributes a rank‑1 binary matrix.\n\nSuppose a tiling uses \\(t\\) tiles with indicator matrices \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_t\\). Since the tiles are disjoint (no unit square is covered twice), the Boolean sum (entrywise OR) of the \\(M_k\\) equals the matrix \\(A\\) of covered cells:\n\\[\nA = M_1\\;\\circ\\;M_2\\;\\circ\\;\\dots\\;\\circ\\;M_t .\n\\]\nThus \\(\\operatorname{br}(A)\\le t\\). By Lemma 2.2 and Lemma 2.1,\n\\[\nt\\;\\ge\\;\\operatorname{br}(A)\\;\\ge\\;\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(A)\n   \\;=\\;\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(J-I)\n   \\;=\\;n .\n\\]\nTherefore any admissible tiling must contain at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n#### 3. Construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) define the rectangle\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{i\\}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\\(R_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines. The collection \\(\\{R_1,\\dots ,R_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) pairwise disjoint tiles; each tile covers all cells of its row except the diagonal cell, and no tile covers any diagonal cell. Consequently every non‑diagonal cell is covered exactly once, and each column also has precisely one uncovered cell (its diagonal entry). Hence this tiling satisfies all requirements with exactly \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n#### 4. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown that any tiling satisfying the problem’s conditions needs at least \\(n\\) tiles, and we have exhibited a tiling that uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore fin