quires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n\n     *Proof.* Order the columns so that \\(c_1>c_2>\\dots >c_n=0\\).  \n     For each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n     \\[\n     S_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid c_{k+1}<i\\le c_k\\}.\n     \\]  \n     The sets \\(S_k\\) are non‑empty and pairwise disjoint.  \n     Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\) covers a cell of \\(S_k\\). If \\(R\\) used any column \\(j>k\\), then its bottom side would be at most \\(c_{k+1}\\) (the height of column \\(j\\)), contradicting the fact that \\(R\\) contains a cell with row \\(>c_{k+1}\\) in column \\(k\\). Hence \\(R\\) uses only columns \\(1,\\dots ,k\\). Because the rows of \\(S_{k'}\\) for \\(k'\\neq k\\) lie either entirely above \\(c_k\\) (if \\(k'<k\\)) or at most \\(c_{k+1}\\) (if \\(k'>k\\)), a rectangle confined to columns \\(\\le k\\) cannot cover any \\(S_{k'}\\) with \\(k'\\neq k\\). Thus each \\(S_k\\) forces a distinct rectangle, giving at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. ∎\n\n   - Applying the lemma to \\(R^{\\uparrow}\\) (whose column heights are the distinct numbers \\(h_j\\)) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed to cover the upper region.  \n   - Applying the lemma to \\(R^{\\downarrow}\\) (distinct depths \\(d_j\\)) shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed to cover the lower region.\n\n   - Any tile cannot contain a hole, so it must lie entirely in the upper region or entirely in the lower region; the two regions are disjoint. Consequently any tiling of the whole board (except the holes) uses at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower‑bound argument proves that no tiling can use fewer. For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA rectangular tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; tiles may not overlap and each unit square may be covered by at most one tile.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nEach of the \\(n\\) rows must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, and each of the \\(n\\) columns must contain exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nTherefore the set of uncovered squares is a set of \\(n\\) squares with pairwise distinct rows and distinct columns; consequently there exists a unique permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nq(j)=p^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\nbe the row index of the uncovered square in column \\(j\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) define  \n\n\\[\nh_j:=q(j)-1\\qquad (0\\le h_j\\le n-1),\\qquad\nd_j:=n-q(j)\\qquad (0\\le d_j\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nThe cells **above** the hole in column \\(j\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le h_j\\},\n\\]\n\nand the cells **below** the hole are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid q(j)+1\\le i\\le n\\}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le d_j\\}.\n\\]\n\nPlace, for every column \\(j\\) with \\(h_j>0\\), a vertical \\(h_j\\times1\\) tile covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le h_j\\).  \nPlace, for every column \\(j\\) with \\(d_j>0\\), a vertical \\(d_j\\times1\\) tile covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(q(j)+1\\le i\\le n\\).\n\nAll tiles lie in distinct columns, hence they are pairwise disjoint.  \nExactly one column has \\(q(j)=1\\) (so \\(h_j=0\\) and no tile above) and exactly one column has \\(q(j)=n\\) (so \\(d_j=0\\) and no tile below).  \nTherefore the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{j\\mid h_j>0\\}\\bigr|+\\bigl|\\{j\\mid d_j>0\\}\\bigr|\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\n##### 3.1  Decomposition into upper and lower regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\uparrow}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<q(j)\\},\n\\qquad\nR^{\\dow