onsequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed. ∎\n\n##### 3.4  Application of the lemma  \n\nApply the lemma to the upper region \\(R^{\\uparrow}\\). Its column heights are the distinct numbers \\(h_j\\); therefore any tiling of \\(R^{\\uparrow}\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nApply the lemma to the lower region \\(R^{\\downarrow}\\). Its column depths are the distinct numbers \\(d_j\\); thus any tiling of \\(R^{\\downarrow}\\) also requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nSince every tile belongs to exactly one of the two regions, any tiling of the whole board (except the uncovered squares) uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ge 2n-2.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Determination of the minimum  \n\nFrom Sections 2 and 3 we have  \n\n\\[\n2n-2\\le \\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2.\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum equals \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that the Summary contains a clear verdict and method sketch, and that the Detailed Solution presents a complete, gap‑free proof using only rigorous arguments. Ensure all mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters and that the final answer is boxed. No extraneous commentary or alternative approaches should appear."
