ith pairwise distinct rows and distinct columns; consequently there exists a unique permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nq(j)=p^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\nbe the row index of the uncovered square in column \\(j\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) define  \n\n\\[\nh_j:=q(j)-1\\qquad (0\\le h_j\\le n-1),\\qquad\nd_j:=n-q(j)\\qquad (0\\le d_j\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nThe cells **above** the hole in column \\(j\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le h_j\\},\n\\]\n\nand the cells **below** the hole are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid q(j)+1\\le i\\le n\\}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le d_j\\}.\n\\]\n\nPlace, for every column \\(j\\) with \\(h_j>0\\), a vertical \\(h_j\\times1\\) tile covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le h_j\\).  \nPlace, for every column \\(j\\) with \\(d_j>0\\), a vertical \\(d_j\\times1\\) tile covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(q(j)+1\\le i\\le n\\).\n\nAll tiles lie in distinct columns, hence they are pairwise disjoint.  \nExactly one column has \\(q(j)=1\\) (so \\(h_j=0\\) and no tile above) and exactly one column has \\(q(j)=n\\) (so \\(d_j=0\\) and no tile below).  \nTherefore the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{j\\mid h_j>0\\}\\bigr|+\\bigl|\\{j\\mid d_j>0\\}\\bigr|\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\n##### 3.1  Decomposition into upper and lower regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\uparrow}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<q(j)\\},\n\\qquad\nR^{\\downarrow}=\\{(i,j)\\mid q(j)<i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery tile must avoid the uncovered squares; consequently a tile cannot contain squares both above and below a hole in the same column, for otherwise the rectangle would contain the hole. Hence each tile lies entirely in \\(R^{\\uparrow}\\) or entirely in \\(R^{\\downarrow}\\). The two regions are disjoint.\n\n##### 3.2  Column heights of the regions  \n\nThe column heights of \\(R^{\\uparrow}\\) are \\(h_j=q(j)-1\\).  \nSince \\(\\{q(j)\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\}=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the multiset \\(\\{h_j\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); thus the heights are all distinct.\n\nSimilarly, the column depths of \\(R^{\\downarrow}\\) are \\(d_j=n-q(j)\\), and \\(\\{d_j\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); the depths are also all distinct.\n\n##### 3.3  Lemma (Ferrers‑diagram bound)  \n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet a Ferrers diagram have column heights \\(c_1>c_2>\\dots >c_n=0\\) (all distinct). Any partition of its cells into axis‑parallel rectangles requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nOrder the columns so that \\(c_1>c_2>\\dots >c_n=0\\).  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid c_{k+1}<i\\le c_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_k\\) is non‑empty and the sets \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \n\nConsider a rectangle \\(R\\) that contains a cell of \\(S_k\\).  \nIf \\(R\\) used any column \\(j>k\\), then the height of column \\(j\\) is at most \\(c_{k+1}\\); consequently the bottom side of \\(R\\) would be at most row \\(c_{k+1}\\), contradicting the presence of a cell of \\(S_k\\) (which lies in a row \\(>c_{k+1}\\)). Hence \\(R\\) uses only columns \\(1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n\nBecause the rows of \\(S_{k'}\\) for \\(k'\\neq k\\) lie either entirely above row \\(c_k\\) (if \\(k'<k\\)) or at most \\(c_{k+1}\\) (if \\(k'>k\\)), a rectangle confined to columns \\(\\le k\\) cannot contain any cell of \\(S_{k'}\\) with \\(k'\\neq k\\). Thus each set \\(S_k\\) forces a distinct rectangle. Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed. ∎\n\n##### 3.4  Application of the lemma  \n\nApply the lemma to the upper region \\(R^{\\uparrow}\\). Its column heights are the distinct numbers \\(h_j\\); therefore any tiling of \\(R^{\\uparrow}\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nApply the lemma to the lower region \\(R^{\\downarrow}\\). Its column depths are the distinct numbers \\(d_j\\); thus any tiling of \\(R^{\\downarrow}\\) also requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nSince every tile belongs to exactly one of the two regions, any tiling of the whole board (except the uncovered squares) uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ge 2n-2.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Determination of the minimum  \n\nFrom Sections 2 and 3 we have  \n\n\\[\n2n-2\\le \\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2.\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum equals \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that the Summary contains a clear verdict and method sketch, and that the Detailed Solution presents a complete, gap‑free proof using only rigorous arguments. Ensure all mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters and that the final answer is boxed. No extraneous commentary or alternative approaches should appear.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, 