1,2,0] requires at least 3 rectangles.\n\nLet's try shape [3,0,2,1] (col1=3, col2=0, col3=2, col4=1). Cells: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1); (1,3),(2,3); (1,4). Could we tile with 2 rectangles? Row1 across columns1,3,4 not contiguous. So need at least two rectangles for row1. So at least 2. Then leftover cells: (2,1),(3,1),(2,3). Could we combine (2,1) and (2,3) in a rectangle? No due to missing column2. So need at least another rectangle. So total at least 3.\n\nThus seems plausible that any histogram with distinct heights (including zero column anywhere) requires at least n-1 rectangles. Let's try to prove: For any histogram with n columns and distinct heights (including zero), the minimal number of rectangles needed is at least n-1. This is plausible because each rectangle can cover at most one \"new\" column at its highest row. Actually we can consider the set of cells that are the highest cell in each column (i.e., the cell at row h_j). These n-1 cells (since one column has height 0, no highest cell) are all at distinct rows (since heights distinct). Each rectangle can contain at most one of these highest cells, because if a rectangle contains two highest cells from columns i and j, then the rectangle's top row must be at least the max of those two rows, but the rectangle's bottom row must be at most the min of the heights of those columns, which is the lower of the two heights, which is less than the higher one, so the rectangle cannot contain the higher cell. More formally: Suppose columns i and j have heights h_i > h_j. The highest cell in column i is at row h_i. Any rectangle covering that cell must have its top row <= h_i and bottom row >= h_i (since it's a single cell). If the rectangle also includes column j, its height cannot exceed h_j (since column j doesn't have cells above h_j). So the rectangle cannot include row h_i > h_j. So rectangle cannot contain both highest cells. Thus each highest cell must be in a distinct rectangle. There are n-1 highest cells (since 