n I\\) we would have both a column \\(j_1\\in J\\) with \\(j_1>\\sigma(i)\\) and a column \\(j_2\\in J\\) with \\(j_2<\\sigma(i)\\); consequently \\(\\sigma(i)\\in J\\), i.e. the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) would belong to \\(T\\), contradicting the requirement that tiles avoid uncovered squares.  \nHence **every tile lies entirely in \\(R\\) or entirely in \\(L\\).**  \nConsequently the total number of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}= \\#\\text{tiles covering }R\\;+\\;\\#\\text{tiles covering }L.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Upper bound – an explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) define the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{\\sigma(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n\\(R_i\\) consists exactly of the cells of row \\(i\\) that lie to the right of the hole; it is a valid tile because it contains no hole.  \nThere are precisely \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) (the unique row with \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) has an empty right part), so the family \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nSimilarly, for each column \\(j\\) with \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)>1\\) define the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nC_j=\\{\\sigma^{-1}(j)+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(C_j\\) consists of the cells of column \\(j\\) that lie below its hole; it is also a legal tile.  \nExactly one column (the column whose hole is in row \\(1\\)) has \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)=1\\) and therefore contributes no vertical tile, so we obtain again \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nThe sets \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{C_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint: a cell of the board is either to the right of its hole (hence belongs to a unique \\(R_i\\)) or to the left of its hole (hence belongs to a unique \\(C_j\\)).  \nTogether they cover all cells of \\(R\\cup L\\) by (1).  \nThus we have a tiling with  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ntiles. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum}\\le 2n-2.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound – at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for \\(R\\)\n\nConsider the set \\(R\\) equipped with the product partial order  \n\n\\[\n(i,j)\\preceq(i',j')\\iff i\\le i'\\ \\text{and}\\ j\\le j'.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nA rectangle \\(I\\times J\\subseteq R\\) is precisely a **chain** of this poset (all its elements are mutually comparable).  \nHence a partition of \\(R\\) into rectangles is a **chain decomposition** of \\((R,\\preceq)\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) the rightmost cell of that row is \\((i,n)\\).  \nCollect these cells:\n\n\\[\nA_R=\\{(i,n)\\mid \\sigma(i)<n\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause the row lengths \\(n-\\sigma(i)\\) are all distinct (they are the numbers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) in some order), the set \\(A_R\\) contains exactly one cell from each non‑empty row of \\(R\\); therefore \\(|A_R|=n-1\\).\n\nIf \\((i,n),(i',n)\\in A_R\\) with \\(i\\neq i'\\), then neither \\((i,n)\\preceq(i',n)\\) nor \\((i',n)\\preceq(i,n)\\) holds (the column coordinates are equal while the row coordinates are incomparable).  \nThus **any two distinct elements of \\(A_R\\) are incomparable**, i.e. \\(A_R\\) is an **antichain** of size \\(n-1\\).\n\nBy **Dilworth’s theorem** (the size of a minimum chain decomposition equals the size of a maximum antichain), any chain decomposition of \\((R,\\preceq)\\) needs at least \\(|A_R|=n-1\\) chains.  \nSince chains are exactly the rectangles covering \\(R\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }R\\;\\ge\\;n-1.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound – the left region  \n\nThe left region \\(L\\) is the transpose of \\(R\\); applying the same argument (or simply swapping the roles of rows and columns) yields\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }L\\;\\ge\\;n-1.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Global lower bound  \n\nFrom (2), (9) and (10),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe upper bound (6) and the lower bound (11) coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
