eft tile) and one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) (no right tile); all other rows contribute two tiles.  \n   - Hence the total number of tiles equals \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   - The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the construction achieving it coincide, so the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   - For \\(n=2025\\) this gives \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(C=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\) the set of squares that have to be covered.\nFor a cell \\((i,j)\\in C\\) we have either \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\) (the cell lies left of the hole in row \\(i\\))\nor \\(j>\\sigma(i)\\) (the cell lies right of that hole).  Define\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThus \\(C=L\\cup R\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Rectangles stay on one side)\n\nLet \\(T\\) be a rectangle (axis‑aligned, with sides on grid lines) that is contained in \\(C\\).\nIf \\(T\\) contains a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\) then every column of \\(T\\) is \\(\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\);\nhence all cells of \\(T\\) satisfy \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\).  Consequently \\(T\\subseteq L\\).\nSimilarly, if \\(T\\) contains a cell with \\(j>\\sigma(i)\\) then \\(T\\subseteq R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose \\(T\\) contains \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\) and also a column\n\\(c\\) of \\(T\\) with \\(c\\ge\\sigma(i)\\).  Since the columns of a rectangle form a\ncontiguous interval, the interval would contain the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\