nd thus the\ncell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), which belongs to \\(U\\), contradicting \\(T\\subseteq C\\).\nThe second statement is symmetric. ∎\n\nHence every tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).  Let\n\\(\\mathcal B_L\\) (resp. \\(\\mathcal B_R\\)) be the family of tiles that lie in \\(L\\) (resp. \\(R\\)).\nThe total number of tiles is \\(|\\mathcal B_L|+|\\mathcal B_R|\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (At least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for \\(L\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(1\\le d\\le n-1\\) let \\(i_d\\) be the unique row satisfying\n\\(\\sigma(i_d)=d+1\\) (such a row exists because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation).\nThe cells\n\\[\n(i_d,1),\\ (i_d,2),\\dots ,\\ (i_d,d)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nbelong to \\(L\\).\n\n*Claim.*  Any tile covering any cell of (3) must have its rightmost column equal to \\(d\\).\n\n*Proof of claim.*  Let \\(T\\in\\mathcal B_L\\) contain \\((i_d,j)\\) with \\(j\\le d\\).\nSince \\(T\\subseteq L\\), Lemma 1 implies that all columns of \\(T\\) are \\(\\le\\sigma(i_d)-1=d\\).\nIf the rightmost column of \\(T\\) were \\(<d\\), then the cell \\((i_d,d)\\) would not be covered,\ncontradicting the fact that (3) must be completely tiled.  Hence the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(d\\). ∎\n\nConsequently, for each \\(d\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there exists at least one tile of \\(\\mathcal B_L\\) whose\nrightmost column is \\(d\\).  Different values of \\(d\\) require different tiles, because a single\ntile has a unique rightmost column.  Hence\n\\[\n|\\mathcal B_L|\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (At least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for \\(R\\))\n\nApply the same reasoning to the right side.  For each \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n\\) let\n\\(j_d\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(j_d)=d-1\\).  The cells\n\\[\n(j_d,d),\\ (j_d,d+1),\\dots ,\\ (j_d,n)\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nlie in \\(R\\).  Any tile covering a cell of (5) must have its leftmost column equal to \\(d\\);\notherwise it would either miss a required cell or contain the hole \\((j_d,d-1)\\).\nThus for each \\(d\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is a distinct tile of \\(\\mathcal B_R\\) whose leftmost column is \\(d\\).\nTherefore\n\\[\n|\\mathcal B_R|\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for the whole board\n\nFrom (4) and (6) we obtain\n\\[\n\\text{(number of tiles)}=|\\mathcal B_L|+|\\mathcal B_R|\n\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nFix any permutation \\(\\sigma\\).  For each row \\(i\\) do the following.\n\n* If \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\), place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times(\\sigma(i)-1)\\) covering the squares\n\\[\n(i,1),\\ (i,2),\\dots ,\\ (i,\\sigma(i)-1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nThese are exactly the cells of \\(L\\) in row \\(i\\).\n\n* If \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\), place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times(n-\\sigma(i))\\) covering the squares\n\\[\n(i,\\sigma(i)+1),\\ (i,\\sigma(i)+2),\\dots ,\\ (i,n).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nThese are exactly the cells of \\(R\\) in row \\(i\\).\n\nAll tiles lie in distinct rows, so they are pairwise disjoint.  The uncovered squares are precisely the\n\\(n\\) squares \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) from (1).  The number of tiles used equals\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\sigma(i)>1\\}}+\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\sigma(i)<n\\}}\\bigr)\n= (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 ,\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nbecause exactly one row satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) (no left tile) and exactly one row satisfies\n\\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) (no right tile).\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (7) and the explicit construction (10) coincide; therefore the minimum possible\nnumber of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  Substituting \\(