)>1\\).  The cells of \\(U_j\\) are exactly the rows \\(1,\\dots ,r(j)-1\\).  If a single upper tile covered column \\(j\\) with height \\(b<r(j)-1\\), the rows \\(b+1,\\dots ,r(j)-1\\) would stay uncovered, and they could not be covered by any other upper tile (any other upper tile covering column \\(j\\) would also start at row 1 and thus overlap).  Hence the tile covering column \\(j\\) must have height \\(b=r(j)-1\\).  Because the height of an upper tile is the same for all columns in its interval, two different columns could share a tile only if they have the same value of \\(r(j)\\).  Since the numbers \\(r(1),\\dots ,r(n)\\) are a permutation, this can happen for at most one column.  Consequently **each column with \\(r(j)>1\\) forces a distinct upper tile**.  There are exactly \\(n-1\\) such columns (the unique column with \\(r(j)=1\\) has no squares above its hole).  \n6. **Each column needs its own lower tile.**  The same reasoning applied to the lower region shows that every column with \\(r(j)<n\\) (there are \\(n-1\\) of them) requires a distinct lower tile.  \n7. **Lower bound.**  Any tiling therefore uses at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.  \n8. **Achieving the bound.**  Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\).  Then \\(r(j)=j\\).  For each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the upper tile  \n   \\[\n   \\{1,\\dots ,j-1\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n   \\]\n   a vertical strip of width 1.  For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the lower tile  \n   \\[\n   \\{j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\}.\n   \\]  \n   These are \\(n-1\\) upper and \\(n-1\\) lower tiles, altogether \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and they cover every square except the diagonal holes \\((i,i)\\).  Thus the lower bound is tight.  \n9. **Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\).**  Substituting \\(n=2025\\) gives the minimal number of tiles  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  The board consists of