## Lemma 2 (Shape of an upper tile).  \nIf \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) is an upper tile, then \\(a=1\\).  Analogously, a lower tile satisfies \\(b=n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(a>1\\).  Choose any column \\(j\\in[c,d]\\).  Since \\(R\\subseteq U\\), we have \\(b<r(j)\\).  The rows \\(1,\\dots ,a-1\\) in column \\(j\\) are also free (they belong to \\(U_j\\)).  They must be covered by some other upper tile \\(R'\\) that also uses column \\(j\\).  By Lemma 1, \\(R'\\) must consist of rows \\([a',b']\\) with \\(b'<r(j)\\).  Because rows \\(1,\\dots ,a-1\\) are uncovered by \\(R\\), we need \\(a'\\le 1\\) and \\(b'\\ge a-1\\).  If \\(b'\\ge a\\), then \\(R'\\) and \\(R\\) would overlap in rows \\(a,\\dots ,\\min\\{b,b'\\}\\), which is forbidden.  Hence \\(b'\\le a-1\\), but then rows \\(a,\\dots ,b\\) of column \\(j\\) would remain uncovered.  This contradiction shows that \\(a\\) cannot exceed 1, so \\(a=1\\). ∎\n\n\n\nThus every upper tile has the form  \n\n\\[\n[1,b]\\times[c,d]\\qquad (1\\le b<\\min_{j\\in[c,d]}r(j)),\n\\]\n\nand every lower tile has the form  \n\n\\[\n[a,n]\\times[c,d]\\qquad (\\max_{j\\in[c,d]}r(j)<a\\le n).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 3 (Each column needs its own upper tile).  \nLet \\(j\\) be a column with \\(r(j)>1\\).  In any tiling, the set \\(U_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<r(j)\\}\\) is covered by **exactly one** upper tile, and that tile has height \\(b=r(j)-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(U_j\\) is non‑empty, some upper tile must contain a cell of column \\(j\\).  By Lemma 2 such a tile is of the form \\([1,b]\\times[c,d]\\) with \\(j\\in[c,d]\\) and \\(b<r(j)\\).  If \\(b<r(j)-1\\), then the rows \\(b+1,\\dots ,r(j)-1\\) of column \\(j\\) would stay uncovered.  Any other upper tile covering column \\(j\\) would also have to start at row 1 (Lemma 2), and therefore would overlap the first tile—impossible.  Hence we must have \\(b=r(j)-1\\).  Moreover, if the same tile also covered another column \\(j'\\neq j\\), then we would need \\(r(j')-1=b\\) as well, i.e. \\(r(j')=r(j)\\), contradicting the fact that the numbers \\(r(1),\\dots ,r(n)\\) are a permutation.  Therefore the tile covering column \\(j\\) is unique to that column. ∎\n\n\n\nSince exactly one column (the one with \\(r(j)=1\\)) has no squares above its hole, Lemma 3 shows that **at least \\(n-1\\) upper tiles** are necessary.  \n\nThe same argument applied to the lower region \\(L\\) yields:\n\n#### Lemma 4 (Each column needs its own lower tile).  \nFor every column \\(j\\) with \\(r(j)<n\\) the set \\(L_j\\) is covered by a unique lower tile of height \\(n-r(j)\\).  Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) lower tiles are required.\n\n---\n\n#### Lower bound\n\nFrom Lemmas 3 and 4 any tiling of the board must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (so the holes are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\)).  Then \\(r(j)=j\\).  \n\n*Upper tiles.*  For each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the tile  \n\n\\[\nR_j^{\\text{up}}=[1,j-1]\\times\\{j\\},\n\\]\n\na vertical strip of width 1 covering exactly the squares above the hole \\((j,j)\\).\n\n*Lower tiles.*  For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the tile  \n\n\\[\nR_j^{\\text{down}}=[j+1,n]\\times\\{j\\},\n\\]\n\na vertical strip covering the squares below the hole \\((j,j)\\).\n\nThese are \\(n-1\\) upper and \\(n-1\\) lower tiles, altogether \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  They are pairwise disjoint, they cover every square of the board except the diagonal holes, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \n\nThus the lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n#### Final computation  \n\nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
