nfiguration  \n\nA rectangular tile is a set  \n\n\\[\nR=[a,b]\\times[c,d]=\\{(i,j)\\mid a\\le i\\le b,\\;c\\le j\\le d\\},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).\n\nIf the intervals \\([a,b]\\) and \\([c,d]\\) intersect, then there exists an integer\n\\(x\\) with \\(a\\le x\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le x\\le d\\); consequently the diagonal cell \\((x,x)\\) belongs to \\(R\\), contradicting the fact that no tile may contain a hole.  \n\nTherefore the intervals are disjoint, and exactly one of the following holds:\n\n\\[\n\\text{(U)}\\qquad b<c\\quad\\text{(the tile lies completely above the diagonal),}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{(L)}\\qquad d<a\\quad\\text{(the tile lies completely below the diagonal).}\n\\]\n\nThus every tile belongs either to the “upper” family (type U) or to the “lower” family (type L).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Lower bound – cells just above and just below the diagonal  \n\nDefine the two sets  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\qquad\nT=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) consists of the cells immediately above the diagonal, \\(T\\) of those immediately below it.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA tile of type U can contain at most one element of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a type U tile be \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) with \\(b<c\\).  \nAssume that \\(R\\) contains two distinct cells \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) from \\(S\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\na\\le i\\le b,\\qquad c\\le i+1\\le d,\n\\]\n\\[\na\\le j\\le b,\\qquad c\\le j+1\\le d.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(i<j\\), we have \\(j\\ge i+1\\). Hence \\(b\\ge j\\ge i+1\\).  \nFrom the first inclusion we also have \\(c\\le i+1\\).  \nThus \\(b\\ge i+1\\ge c\\), contradicting the requirement \\(b<c\\). ∎\n\nBy symmetry the same statement holds for tiles of type L and the set \\(T\\).\n\n---\n\nBecause \\(|S|=n-1\\) and each type U tile covers at most one element of \\(S\\), at least \\(n-1\\) type U tiles are necessary to cover all cells above the diagonal.  \nSimilarly, at least \\(n-1\\) type L tiles are necessary for the cells below the diagonal.\n\nHence any tiling of the board must use at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Construction attaining the lower bound  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{up}}_{j}=[1,j-1]\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince its row interval \\([1,j-1]\\) ends at \\(j-1<b\\) and its column interval is the single column \\(\\{j\\}\\) with \\(c=j\\), we have \\(b=j-1<c=j\\); thus \\(R^{\\text{up}}_{j}\\) is of type U and lies entirely above the diagonal.\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{down}}_{j}=[j+1,n]\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\]\n\nHere the row interval starts at \\(j+1\\) and the column interval is \\(\\{j\\}\\); consequently \\(d=j<a=j+1\\), so \\(R^{\\text{down}}_{j}\\) is of type L and lies entirely below the diagonal.\n\nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}= \\{R^{\\text{up}}_{j}\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n          \\{R^{\\text{down}}_{j}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n*Disjointness.*  \nEach tile occupies a single column; distinct columns are disjoint, so the tiles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nConsider a non‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n- If \\(i<j\\) (the cell is above the diagonal), then \\(j\\ge 2\\) and the tile \\(R^{\\text{up}}_{j}\\) contains all rows \\(1,\\dots ,j-1\\) of column \\(j\\); in particular it contains \\((i,j)\\).\n\n- If \\(i>j\\) (the cell is below the diagonal), then \\(j\\le n-1\\) and the tile \\(R^{\\text{down}}_{j}\\) contains all rows \\(j+1,\\dots ,n\\) of column \\(j\\); thus it contains \\((i,j)\\).\n\nHence every off‑diagonal cell belongs to exactly one tile, while the diagonal cells