ly satisfying (★).  \n   *Base case:* \\(f(2)=2\\) (the two arcs must belong to different cuts).  \n   *Inductive step:* Choose a vertex \\(v\\). To cover the outgoing arcs from \\(v\\) we need at least one cut with \\(v\\in R\\); to cover the incoming arcs we need at least one cut with \\(v\\notin R\\). Removing \\(v\\) and discarding those two cuts leaves a family that still satisfies (★) for the remaining \\(n-1\\) vertices, so  \n   \\[\n   f(n)-2\\ge f(n-1).\n   \\]\n   By the induction hypothesis \\(f(n-1)=2(n-1)-2\\), whence  \n   \\[\n   f(n)\\ge 2n-2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) set  \n   \\[\n   R_i=\\{1,2,\\dots ,i\\},\\qquad\n   R_i'=\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n   The \\(2n-2\\) subsets \\(\\{R_i,R_i'\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) satisfy (★):  \n   - If \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in R_i\\times([n]\\setminus R_i)\\).  \n   - If \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in R_j'\\times([n]\\setminus R_j')\\).  \n   The corresponding rectangles are  \n   \\[\n   [1,i]\\times[i+1,n]\\quad\\text{and}\\quad[i+1,n]\\times[1,i],\n   \\]\n   which tile the whole board except the diagonal holes. Hence \\(f(n)\\le 2n-2\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining (1) with the construction gives \\(f(n)=2n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge2\\).  \n   For the original grid \\(n=2025\\) the minimum number of tiles is  \n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the statement leaves exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the uncovered squares form a *permutation matrix*: there is a bijection \\(\\sigma:[n]\\to[n]\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy permuting rows according to \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) and columns according to the identity we may assume \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e. the holes are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \nAll arguments below are invariant under this relabelling, so we work with this convenient configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Rectangles as directed cuts  \n\nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.  \nIf a rectangle uses the set of rows \\(R\\subseteq[n]\\) and the set of columns \\(C\\subseteq[n]\\), then it covers exactly the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in R,\\ j\\in C\\).  \nBecause the diagonal cells are uncovered, a rectangle may not contain any diagonal cell; therefore  \n\n\\[\nR\\cap C=\\varnothing .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConversely, any pair \\((R,C)\\) of subsets satisfying (1) yields a legal tile.  \n\nThus the collection of tiles is a family \\(\\{(R_k,C_k)\\}_{k=1}^{m}\\) with \\(R_k\\cap C_k=\\varnothing\\) that **partitions** the set  \n\n\\[\nE=\\{(i,j)\\mid i,j\\in[n],\\ i\\neq j\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ni.e. each ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs to exactly one product \\(R_k\\times C_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Algebraic consequences of a partition  \n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\na_k=|R_k|,\\qquad b_k=|C_k| .\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(a_k+b_k\\le n\\).  \nBecause the family partitions (2),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{m}a_k b_k = |E| = n(n-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) the outgoing arcs \\((i,j),\\,j\\neq i\\) must be covered, and a rectangle \\((R_k,C_k)\\) contributes \\(|C_k|=b_k\\) arcs from row \\(i\\) precisely when \\(i\\in R_k\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k:\\,i\\in R_k} b_k = n-1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSummation of (4) over all rows gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{m} a_k (n-a_k)=n(n-1). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously, considering the incoming arcs to a column yields\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{m} b_k (n-b_k)=n(n-1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Every optimal rectangle uses a complementary pair  \n\nFrom (4) we have for each \\(i\\)\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k:\\,i\\in R_k} b_k = n-1 = \\sum_{k:\\,i\\in R_k} (n-a_k) .\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(i\\in R_k\\),\n\n\\[\nb_k = n-a_k .\n\\]\n\nSince the left‑hand side does not depend on \\(i\\), the equality must hold for **all** rectangles that contain any row, i.e.\n\n\\[\nb_k = n-a_k \\qquad\\text{for every }k. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\na_k+b_k=n\\qquad(k=1,\\dots ,m). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSo each rectangle uses a *complementary* pair of subsets: once the row set \\(R_k\\) is chosen, the column set is forced to be its complement \\([n]\\setminus R_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Reformulation as a partition into directed cuts  \n\nBecause of (8) we may identify a rectangle solely by its row set \\(R_k\\).  \nCondition (2) becomes\n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{k=1}^{m} \\bigl(R_k\\times([n]\\setminus R_k)\\bigr)=E,\n\\qquad\nR_i\\times([n]\\setminus R_i)\\cap R_j\\times([n]\\setminus R_j)=\\varnothing\\;(i\\neq j). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus we have partitioned the arcs of the complete digraph \\(K_n^{\\;\\rightarrow}\\) (all ordered pairs with distinct ends) into *directed cuts* determined by the subsets \\(R_k\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\min\\{\\,m\\mid\\text{there exists a family } \\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{m}\\text{ satisfying (9)}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Lower bound for \\(f(n)\\) (induction)  \n\n**Base \\(n=2\\).** The two arcs \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) cannot belong to the same cut, so \\(f(2)=2=2\\cdot2-2\\).\n\n**Inductive step.** Assume \\(f(k)=2k-2\\) for all \\(k<n\\).  \nTake a family \\(\\{R_1,\\dots ,R_m\\}\\) satisfying (9) for the set \\([n]\\).  \nChoose a vertex \\(v\\in[n]\\).\n\n*Outgoing arcs.* The arcs \\((v,j),\\ j\\neq v\\) must belong to some cut with \\(v\\in R_k\\); therefore at least one set \\(R_{k_1}\\) contains \\(v\\).\n\n*Incoming arcs.* The arcs \\((i,v),\\ i\\neq v\\) must belong to a cut with \\(v\\notin R_k\\); hence there is at least one set \\(R_{k_2}\\) that does **not** contain \\(v\\).\n\nDelete the two sets \\(R_{k_1},R_{k_2}\\) and delete the vertex \\(v\\) from all remaining sets.  \nThe remaining family still satisfies (9) on the vertex set \\([n]\\setminus\\{v\\}\\) (every ordered pair not involving \\(v\\) is untouched, and no pair is duplicated).  \nThus we obtain a family of at most \\(m-2\\) cuts for \\(n-1\\) vertices, whence\n\n\\[\nm-2\\ge f(n-1)=2(n-1)-2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\ge 2n-2 .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{1,2,\\dots ,i\\},\\qquad\nR_i'=\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe \\(2n-2\\) subsets  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1},R_1',\\dots ,R_{n-1}'\\}\n\\]\n\nsatisfy (9):  \n\n*If \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in R_i\\times([n]\\setminus R_i)\\).  \n*If \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in R_j'\\times([n]\\setminus R_j')\\).\n\nThe corresponding rectangles are  \n\n\\[\n[1,i]\\times[i+1,n]\\quad\\text{and}\\quad[i+1,n]\\times[1,i],\n\\]\n\nwhich together cover every off‑diagonal cell exactly once.  \nHence \\(f(n)\\le 2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Minimal number of tiles  \n\nFrom (10) and the construction we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=2n-2\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge2.\n\\]\n\nRecalling that each rectangle corresponds to one tile, the minimal number of tiles required for an \\(n\\times n\\) board with one uncovered square per row and column is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
