r  \n\nFrom (4) we have for each \\(i\\)\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k:\\,i\\in R_k} b_k = n-1 = \\sum_{k:\\,i\\in R_k} (n-a_k) .\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(i\\in R_k\\),\n\n\\[\nb_k = n-a_k .\n\\]\n\nSince the left\u2011hand side does not depend on \\(i\\), the equality must hold for **all** rectangles that contain any row, i.e.\n\n\\[\nb_k = n-a_k \\qquad\\text{for every }k. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\na_k+b_k=n\\qquad(k=1,\\dots ,m). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSo each rectangle uses a *complementary* pair of subsets: once the row set \\(R_k\\) is chosen, the column set is forced to be its complement \\([n]\\setminus R_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Reformulation as a partition into directed cuts  \n\nBecause of (8) we may identify a rectangle solely by its row set \\(R_k\\).  \nCondition (2) becomes\n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{k=1}^{m} \\bigl(R_k\\times([n]\\setminus R_k)\\bigr)=E,\n\\qquad\nR_i\\times([n]\\setminus R_i)\\cap R_j\\times([n]\\setminus R_j)=\\varnothing\\;(i\\neq j). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus we have partitioned the arcs of the complete digraph \\(K_n^{\\;\\rightarrow}\\) (all ordered pairs with distinct ends) into *directed cuts* determined by the subsets \\(R_k\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\min\\{\\,m\\mid\\text{there exists a family } \\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{m}\\text{ satisfying (9)}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Lower bound for \\(f(n)\\) (induction)  \n\n**Base \\(n=2\\).** The two arcs \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) cannot belong to the same cut, so \\(f(2)=2=2\\cdot2-2\\).\n\n**Inductive step.** Assume \\(f(k)=2k-2\\) for all \\(k<n\\).  \nTake a family \\(\\{R_1,\\dots ,R_m\\}\\) satisfying (9) for the set \\([n]\\).  \nChoose a vertex \\(v\\in[n]\\).\n\n*Outgoing arcs.* The arcs \\((v,j),\\ j\\neq v\\) must belong to some cut with \\(v\\in R_k\\); therefore at least one set \\(R_{k_1}\\) contains \\(v\\).\n\n*Incoming arcs.* The arcs \\((i,v),\\ i\\neq v\\) must belong to a cut with \\(v\\notin R_k\\); hence there is at least one set \\(R_{k_2}\\) that does **not** contain \\(v\\).\n\nDelete the two sets \\(R_{k_1},R_{k_2}\\) and delete the vertex \\(v\\) from all remaining sets.  \nThe remaining family still satisfies (9) on the vertex set \\([n]\\setminus\\{v\\}\\) (every ordered pair not involving \\(v\\) is untouched, and no pair is duplicated).  \nThus we obtain a family of at most \\(m-2\\) cuts for \\(n-1\\) vertices, whence\n\n\\[\nm-2\\ge f(n-1)=2(n-1)-2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\ge 2n-2 .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{1,2,\\dots ,i\\},\\qquad\nR_i'=\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe \\(2n-2\\) subsets  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1},R_1',\\dots ,R_{n-1}'\\}\n\\]\n\nsatisfy (9):  \n\n*If \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in R_i\\times([n]\\setminus R_i)\\).  \n*If \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in R_j'\\times([n]\\setminus R_j')\\).\n\nThe corresponding rectangles are  \n\n\\[\n[1,i]\\times[i+1,n]\\quad\\text{and}\\quad[i+1,n]\\times[1,i],\n\\]\n\nwhich together cover every off\u2011diagonal cell exactly once.  \nHence \\(f(n)\\le 2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Minimal number of tiles  \n\nFrom (10) and the construction we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=2n-2\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge2.\n\\]\n\nRecalling that each rectangle corresponds to one tile, the minimal number of tiles required for an \\(n\\times n\\) board with one uncovered square per row and column is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
