"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have a complete and rigorous solution. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation reduction.**  \n   - The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \n   - By simultaneously permuting the rows according to \\(\\sigma\\) (or equivalently permuting rows and columns), we may assume without loss of generality that the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\). This transformation does not change the number of tiles.\n\n2. **Lower bound \\(2n-2\\).**  \n   - Define the **outer\u2011corner cells**  \n     \\[\n     c_k^{L}=(k+1,k),\\qquad c_k^{R}=(k,k+1)\\qquad(k=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n     \\]  \n     All these \\(2n-2\\) cells are covered (they are not diagonal).  \n   - **Lemma.** Any axis\u2011parallel rectangle that avoids all diagonal cells can contain **at most one** of the cells \\(\\{c_k^{L},c_k^{R}\\}\\).  \n     *Proof.*  Write a rectangle as \\(I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of rows and columns.  \n     \u2013 If it contained two distinct left\u2011corner cells \\((i+1,i)\\) and \\((j+1,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\), then \\(I\\) would contain rows \\(i+1,\\dots ,j+1\\) and \\(J\\) would contain columns \\(i,\\dots ,j\\); consequently the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) would lie inside, contradiction.  \n     \u2013 The same argument rules out two right\u2011corner cells.  \n     \u2013 If it contained one left\u2011corner \\((i+1,i)\\) and one right\u2011corner \\((j,j+1)\\), then either \\(i\\le j\\) or \\(i>j\\); in both cases the diagonal cell \\((\\max\\{i+1,j\\},\\max\\{i+1,j\\})\\) belongs to the rectangle, again a contradiction. \u220e  \n   - Since each rectangle can cover at most one outer\u2011corner cell, at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are necessary.\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   - Keep the uncovered squares on the diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) place the **row\u2011strip**  \n     \\[\n     R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n     \\]  \n     a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle covering the whole left part of row \\(i\\).  \n   - For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the **column\u2011strip**  \n     \\[\n     C_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n     \\]  \n     a \\((j-1)\\times1\\) rectangle covering the whole upper part of column \\(j\\).  \n   - The \\(R_i\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct rows; the \\(C_j\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct columns.  \n   - A row\u2011strip \\(R_i\\) and a column\u2011strip \\(C_j\\) are disjoint:  \n     \u2013 If \\(i>j\\) then \\(R_i\\) contains column \\(j\\) but only row \\(i\\), while \\(C_j\\) contains row \\(j\\) but not row \\(i\\);  \n     \u2013 If \\(i<j\\) then \\(R_i\\) does not contain column \\(j\\) (its columns are \\(\\le i-1<j\\)).  \n     Hence no cell belongs to both a row\u2011strip and a column\u2011strip.  \n   - Every non\u2011diagonal cell belongs either to a unique row\u2011strip (if it lies below the diagonal) or to a unique column\u2011strip (if it lies above the diagonal). Thus the union of all \\(R_i\\) and \\(C_j\\) covers exactly the set of all squares except the diagonal.  \n   - The total number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   - The lower\u2011bound argument forces any admissible placement to use at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n   - The explicit construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n   - Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \n   - For \\(n=2025\\) this value is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of rectangular tiles satisfying the condition leaves exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column. Consequently there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 1. Reduction to the diagonal case  \n\nConsider the bijection of rows \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) defined by \\(\\pi(i)=\\sigma(i)\\).  \nApply \\(\\pi\\) to the board: move row \\(i\\) to the position \\(\\sigma(i)\\) while leaving the columns unchanged.  \nUnder this transformation a square \\((i,j)\\) moves to \\((\\sigma(i),j)\\).  \nHence each uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) becomes \\((\\sigma(i),\\sigma(i))\\), i.e. a diagonal cell.  \nEvery rectangle is mapped to a rectangle of the same size, and the number of rectangles does not change.  \nTherefore the minimal number of tiles for an arbitrary permutation \\(\\sigma\\) equals the minimal number for the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\).  \nFrom now on we assume  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll diagonal cells are uncovered; every other cell must be covered by exactly one tile.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nDefine the **outer\u2011corner cells**  \n\n\\[\nc_k^{L}=(k+1,k),\\qquad c_k^{R}=(k,k+1)\\qquad(k=1,\\dots ,n-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese are precisely the cells immediately below and immediately to the right of the diagonal.  \nNone of them belongs to \\(U\\); consequently each of them must be covered by some tile.\n\nLet a tile be an axis\u2011parallel rectangle \\(Q=I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are non\u2011empty intervals of row indices and column indices, respectively.  \nBecause the diagonal cells are uncovered, \\(Q\\) cannot contain any cell \\((i,i)\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma.**  \n\\(Q\\) can contain at most one cell from the set \\(\\{c_k^{L},c_k^{R}\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(Q=I\\times J\\) with \\(I=[r_1,r_2]\\) and \\(J=[c_1,c_2]\\) (integers).  \n\n*Case 1 \u2013 two left\u2011corner cells.*  \nSuppose \\(c_i^{L}=(i+1,i)\\) and \\(c_j^{L}=(j+1,j)\\) belong to \\(Q\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThen \\(I\\) contains rows \\(i+1,\\dots ,j+1\\) and \\(J\\) contains columns \\(i,\\dots ,j\\).  \nHence the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) (row \\(i+1\\), column \\(i+1\\)) lies in \\(I\\times J\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(Q\\) avoids all diagonal cells.\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 two right\u2011corner cells.*  \nIf \\(c_i^{R}=(i,i+1)\\) and \\(c_j^{R}=(j,j+1)\\) belong to \\(Q\\) with \\(i<j\\), then \\(I\\) contains rows \\(i,\\dots ,j\\) and \\(J\\) contains columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,j+1\\).  \nThus the diagonal cell \\((j,j)\\) lies in \\(Q\\), again a contradiction.\n\n*Case 3 \u2013 one left\u2011corner and one right\u2011corner cell.*  \nAssume \\(c_i^{L}=(i+1,i)\\in Q\\) and \\(c_j^{R}=(j,j+1)\\in Q\\).  \nIf \\(i\\le j\\), then \\(I\\) contains rows \\(i+1,\\dots ,j\\) and \\(J\\) contains columns \\(i,\\dots ,j+1\\); consequently \\((i+1,i+1)\\in Q\\).  \nIf \\(i>j\\), then \\(I\\) contains rows \\(j,\\dots ,i+1\\) and \\(J\\) contains columns \\(j,\\dots ,i\\); consequently \\((j+1,j+1)\\in Q\\).  \nIn either sub\u2011case a diagonal cell is present, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\nAll possibilities lead to a contradiction, so \\(Q\\) cannot contain two distinct outer\u2011corner cells. \u220e\n\n---\n\nSince there are exactly \\(2n-2\\) outer\u2011corner cells (3) and each tile can cover at most one of them, any admissible tiling must use at least  \n\n\\[\n2n-2 \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nWe keep the uncovered squares on the diagonal as in (2).\n\n**Row\u2011strip tiles.**  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n\\(R_i\\) is a rectangle of height \\(1\\) (row \\(i\\)) and width \\(i-1\\); it lies entirely in the lower\u2011triangular region \\(\\{(i,j):j<i\\}\\) and does not contain any diagonal cell.\n\n**Column\u2011strip tiles.**  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n\\(C_j\\) is a rectangle of width \\(1\\) (column \\(j\\)) and height \\(j-1\\); it lies entirely in the upper\u2011triangular region \\(\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\\) and avoids the diagonal.\n\n**Disjointness.**  \n- The sets \\(R_i\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct rows.  \n- The sets \\(C_j\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct columns.  \n- For a row\u2011strip \\(R_i\\) and a column\u2011strip \\(C_j\\):  \n  *If \\(i>j\\), then \\(R_i\\) contains column \\(j\\) but only row \\(i\\), whereas \\(C_j\\) contains rows up to \\(j-1\\) and not row \\(i\\); thus \\(R_i\\cap C_j=\\varnothing\\).  \n  *If \\(i<j\\), then \\(R_i\\) contains only columns \\(\\le i-1<j\\), so it does not intersect column \\(j\\).  \n  Hence \\(R_i\\cap C_j=\\varnothing\\) for all \\(i,j\\).\n\nTherefore all \\(R_i\\) and \\(C_j\\) are mutually disjoint.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nEvery non\u2011diagonal cell \\((p,q)\\) satisfies either \\(p>q\\) (it lies below the diagonal) or \\(p<q\\) (above the diagonal).  \n- If \\(p>q\\), then \\(p\\ge2\\) and \\(q\\le p-1\\); thus \\((p,q)\\in R_p\\).  \n- If \\(p<q\\), then \\(q\\ge2\\) and \\(p\\le q-1\\); thus \\((p,q)\\in C_q\\).  \nHence the union of all \\(R_i\\) and \\(C_j\\) equals the set of all squares except the diagonal cells \\(U\\).\n\n**Count of tiles.**  \nThere are \\(n-1\\) row\u2011strip tiles (for \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\)) and \\(n-1\\) column\u2011strip tiles (for \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\)), giving a total of  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\nThus we have exhibited a tiling that meets the required condition with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Minimality and final answer\n\nThe lower\u2011bound argument (4) shows that no admissible placement can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nThe construction above achieves this bound. Consequently the mini